site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropolis_of_Pranu_Muttedu]; The menhirs stand in pairs, alignments and groups [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropolis_of_Pranu_Muttedu]; The site has funerary chambers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropolis_of_Pranu_Muttedu]; The funerary chambers were once covered by a tumulus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropolis_of_Pranu_Muttedu]; The funerary chambers are ringed by megalithic circles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Necropolis_of_Pranu_Muttedu]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('06f66762-c2a9-465c-bc9e-a31052ec7ce6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the way to the Acropolis of Athens, the Roman Beulé Gate was built almost entirely from the Choragic Monument of Nikias, whose dedicatory inscription still shows in its entablature.', 'A Roman gate into the Acropolis built almost entirely from recycled stones of a demolished 4th-century BC monument. The original dedicatory inscription is still visible.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:06f66762-c2a9-465c-bc9e-a31052ec7ce6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Roman gate into the Acropolis built almost entirely from recycled stones of a demolished 4th-century BC monument. The original dedicatory inscription is still visible.'' restored on Beulé Gate', '9febbf14719f195c515a2b6605026d1f2a8c2a08f5a87845ca529d52a1c8fcd7|b8e0cdb2911a083434d1d62dbb1740060b4ee8e8cffa4cc7ac95e370904e6b7e|b8e0cdb2911a083434d1d62dbb1740060b4ee8e8cffa4cc7ac95e370904e6b7e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the way to the Acropolis of Athens, the Roman Beulé Gate was built almost entirely from the Choragic Monument of Nikias, whose dedicatory inscription still shows in its entablature."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-001 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Beulé Gate leads to the Acropolis of Athens [S1]; it was built in the Roman period [S1]; it was built almost entirely from materials of the Choragic Monument of Nikias [S2]; the dedicatory inscription of Nikias''s monument is still visible in the gate''s entablature [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-003 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Beulé Gate is a gate on the approach (the way up) to the Acropolis of Athens [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beul%C3%A9_Gate]; The Beulé Gate is Roman, i.e. built in the Roman period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beul%C3%A9_Gate]; The gate was built almost entirely from the material of the Choragic Monument of Nikias [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beul%C3%A9_Gate]; The dedicatory inscription of the Choragic Monument of Nikias is still visible in the entablature of the Beulé Gate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beul%C3%A9_Gate]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0849cdd5-5b9a-40c2-9aa9-f58c1ac32b22'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1900 Harriet Boyd found 13 rooms on Kavousi Kastro and took them for an early chieftain''s house. Now they read as separate homes on the terraces of a Greek Dark Ages refuge.', 'The Kastro is best known as a defensible “refuge” settlement of the “Greek Dark Ages”, inhabited from the early 12th to the mid-7th centuries BCE.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0849cdd5-5b9a-40c2-9aa9-f58c1ac32b22:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The Kastro is best known as a defensible “refuge” settlement of the “Greek Dark Ages”, inhabited from the early 12th to the mid-7th centuries BCE.'' restored on Kavousi Kastro', 'da317b274868a536105ee56adcdb958caf00f8d7875de1d35d3fb579eb1aeada|dacf01526ae4504631ff3429f4c10b6283a0b60a5d6c37d172e5d540b4266e4f|dacf01526ae4504631ff3429f4c10b6283a0b60a5d6c37d172e5d540b4266e4f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1900 Harriet Boyd found 13 rooms on Kavousi Kastro and took them for an early chieftain''s house. Now they read as separate homes on the terraces of a Greek Dark Ages refuge."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site was explored in 1900 [S5]; the explorer was Harriet Boyd [S5]; she found 13 rooms on Kavousi Kastro [S5]; she took the rooms for the house of an early chieftain [S5]; the rooms are now interpreted as separate homes (house complexes) [S5]; the homes lie on terraces [S5]; Kavousi Kastro was a refuge settlement of the Greek Dark Ages [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-004 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kavousi Kastro is a refuge settlement [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; The site is in Greece [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; The refuge belongs to the Greek Dark Ages [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; Harriet Boyd worked on Kavousi Kastro in 1900 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; Boyd found 13 rooms on the Kastro [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; Boyd took the rooms for an early chieftain''s house [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; The rooms are now interpreted as separate homes (house complexes) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]; The houses lie on terraces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kavousi_Kastro]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('086aa9b8-6e6e-492d-baf2-a829b5eb0f6c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a small hill above the Aln valley, Jenny''s Lantern keeps an Iron Age hillfort and the remains of at least 15 stone-founded circular huts. A lacrymatory was found here in 1824.', 'A hillfort from around 700 BC was later overbuilt by a Roman-era stone settlement with an attached field system on this Northumberland moorland. Two occupations share the same defensible ground.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:086aa9b8-6e6e-492d-baf2-a829b5eb0f6c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A hillfort from around 700 BC was later overbuilt by a Roman-era stone settlement with an attached field system on this Northumberland moorland. Two occupations share the same defensible ground.'' restored on Jenny''s Lantern', '8eb310ac59841cbed0f32aa8af88ef3fa6d9428874a3fb9478b568b985fb608b|ddf418e968ba72020534c38222275829ae90ce6f50e1957db1eaa7a57f78192a|ddf418e968ba72020534c38222275829ae90ce6f50e1957db1eaa7a57f78192a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a small hill above the Aln valley, Jenny''s Lantern keeps an Iron Age hillfort and the remains of at least 15 stone-founded circular huts. A lacrymatory was found here in 1824."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Jenny''s Lantern is a small hill [S2]; the hill rises above the Aln valley [S2]; Jenny''s Lantern has an Iron Age hillfort [S1, S3]; remains of at least 15 huts are there [S4]; the huts are stone-founded [S4]; the huts are circular [S4]; a lacrymatory was found at the site [S6]; the lacrymatory was found in 1824 [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-004 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Jenny''s Lantern is the site of an Iron Age hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; Jenny''s Lantern is in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; It lies on a small hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The hill stands above the Aln valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The site keeps the remains of at least 15 huts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The huts are stone-founded [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The huts are circular [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; A lacrymatory was found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]; The lacrymatory was found in 1824 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jenny%27s_Lantern]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('08882f84-f34c-4930-b595-ef7e46a6916d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On an isolated hilltop in Exmoor, the Iron Age hillfort of Cow Castle commands the valley of the River Barle behind a single rampart and ditch. What threatens it now is bracken.', 'An Iron Age hilltop fort that may never have been finished. Its earthworks enclose just 0.9 hectares — adapted to the terrain of an isolated hilltop.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:08882f84-f34c-4930-b595-ef7e46a6916d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''An Iron Age hilltop fort that may never have been finished. Its earthworks enclose just 0.9 hectares — adapted to the terrain of an isolated hilltop.'' restored on Cow Castle', 'd7717aa110a1d9bcc4e3f87aa9f5b11bd481b09e63e6cf980621f1314e3aa6e7|fa4c0c8713d7b710cf8c884857cabae20869bbc4c205112349a7e3e36b2506be|fa4c0c8713d7b710cf8c884857cabae20869bbc4c205112349a7e3e36b2506be', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On an isolated hilltop in Exmoor, the Iron Age hillfort of Cow Castle commands the valley of the River Barle behind a single rampart and ditch. What threatens it now is bracken."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cow Castle is on an isolated hilltop [S4]; it lies in Exmoor [S1]; it is an Iron Age hillfort [S1]; it commands the valley of the River Barle [S4]; it has a single rampart and ditch [S4]; it is threatened now by bracken [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-004 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cow Castle is a hillfort in Exmoor, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It lies in Exmoor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It is in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; The hillfort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It stands on an isolated hilltop [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It commands the valley of the River Barle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It has a single rampart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]; It has a ditch [SUPPORTED: https://www.exmoorher.co.uk/Monument/MSO6797]; The site is under threat now [SUPPORTED: https://www.exmoorher.co.uk/Monument/MSO6797]; The threat to it is bracken [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cow_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('093a4419-157b-4562-8da5-6be601aabc2d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a ridge 3480 meters up, Raqch''i was a control point on the Inca road system from Cusco. Its Temple of Wiracocha, the biggest kallanka of the Inca Empire, stands by 152 round qullqas.', 'The Inka site at Raqch''i was a primary control point on a road system that originated in Cusco and expanded as the Inka empire grew.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:093a4419-157b-4562-8da5-6be601aabc2d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "The Inka site at Raqch''i was a primary control point on a road system that originated in Cusco and expanded as the Inka empire grew." restored on Raqch''i', '7b28f37b9a66d7cf58608ad2567dd3fb412c8c4ce8f7a0a1ed61374f0085acee|3a6189e046260b16b08f6ced57843ad0d37c9959c7be67be61f7c1995b15efd9|3a6189e046260b16b08f6ced57843ad0d37c9959c7be67be61f7c1995b15efd9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a ridge 3480 meters up, Raqch''i was a control point on the Inca road system from Cusco. Its Temple of Wiracocha, the biggest kallanka of the Inca Empire, stands by 152 round qullqas."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raqch''i lies on a ridge [S4]; it is 3480 meters above sea level [S2]; it was a control point on the Inca road system [S3]; that road system originated in Cusco [S3]; it has a Temple of Wiracocha [S5]; the Temple of Wiracocha is the biggest kallanka of the Inca Empire [S5, S6]; the temple stands beside the qullqas [S8]; there are 152 qullqas [S8]; the qullqas are round [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-004 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Raqch''i is an Inca site in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; It lies on a ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; It lies 3480 meters above sea level [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; It was a control point [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; It lay on the Inca road system [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; That road system ran from Cusco [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; The site has a Temple of Wiracocha [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; The Temple of Wiracocha is the biggest kallanka of the Inca Empire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kallanka]; The temple stands by round qullqas (storehouses) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]; There are 152 of these qullqas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raqch%27i]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('097bd79b-a0d9-40d1-832d-c8a0671f7ab3'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Next to Bhir Mound, just south of Sirkap in the Taxila area, lies Hathial, quite a large ancient site. The red burnished ware and other materials found here resemble those of Charsadda.', 'Settlement at Taxila dating to 1000 BC, predating the adjacent Bhir Mound by five centuries. Red burnished ware matching Charsadda pottery reveals deep connections across early Gandharan culture.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:097bd79b-a0d9-40d1-832d-c8a0671f7ab3:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Settlement at Taxila dating to 1000 BC, predating the adjacent Bhir Mound by five centuries. Red burnished ware matching Charsadda pottery reveals deep connections across early Gandharan culture.'' restored on Hathial', '902affaa13fc47d3d310c00b2aa346cf7f0fad9e6fb993f3c29549daa780f0f2|88f76a40b3d2747e5587d26a83bd3eca9c2499e23127a279851bce223b688881|88f76a40b3d2747e5587d26a83bd3eca9c2499e23127a279851bce223b688881', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Next to Bhir Mound, just south of Sirkap in the Taxila area, lies Hathial, quite a large ancient site. The red burnished ware and other materials found here resemble those of Charsadda."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hathial lies next to Bhir Mound [S1]; Hathial lies just south of Sirkap [S1]; Hathial is in the Taxila area [S1]; Hathial is an ancient site [S1]; Hathial is quite a large site [S2]; red burnished ware was found here [S2]; other materials were found here [S2]; the red burnished ware and other materials resemble those of Charsadda [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-004 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hathial is an ancient archaeological site in the Taxila area of Pakistan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; It lies next to Bhir Mound [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taxila]; It lies just south of Sirkap [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; It is in the Taxila area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; It is quite a large site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; Red burnished ware was found here [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; Other materials were found here [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]; The red burnished ware and other finds resemble those of Charsadda [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hathial]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0a7b9508-615a-47ac-8044-abdae7a7401b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Roman road Dere Street ran north from York across Hadrian''s Wall into what is now Scotland. At Dun Law it was laid over a palaeochannel on a lattice of logs and a mat of branchwood.', 'A Roman road running north from York, crossing Hadrian''s Wall and continuing into modern-day northern territory. Portions are still followed by modern highways.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0a7b9508-615a-47ac-8044-abdae7a7401b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "A Roman road running north from York, crossing Hadrian''s Wall and continuing into modern-day northern territory. Portions are still followed by modern highways." restored on Dere Street', '8ecca09cb7003d1791438b49e779dffad1c199a1204cf7b7fdb44adde2340b6c|4aa17100d323c0a61b5e89f377fbc24564ae3aa5be7d2f7c91845f9ccf7fdc34|4aa17100d323c0a61b5e89f377fbc24564ae3aa5be7d2f7c91845f9ccf7fdc34', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman road Dere Street ran north from York across Hadrian''s Wall into what is now Scotland. At Dun Law it was laid over a palaeochannel on a lattice of logs and a mat of branchwood."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dere Street is a Roman road [S1]; it ran north from York [S1]; it crossed Hadrian''s Wall [S1]; it continued into what is now Scotland [S1]; a section of the road lies at Dun Law [S7]; at Dun Law it was laid over a palaeochannel [S7, S8]; it was laid on a lattice of logs [S8]; it was laid on a mat of branchwood [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Dere Street is a Roman road [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; Dere Street ran north from York [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; Dere Street crossed Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; Dere Street continued into what is now Scotland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; A section of Dere Street lies at Dun Law [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; At Dun Law the road was laid over a palaeochannel [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; At Dun Law the road was laid on a lattice of logs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]; At Dun Law the road was laid on a mat of branchwood [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dere_Street]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0abf05cb-0fda-4567-9d9d-0829911a8c66'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1963 two students found Paradise Cave inside Malik hill, south of Kielce. Its cave deposits hold traces of Neanderthal occupancy from the late Pleistocene, 50 to 60 thousand years ago.', 'In the sub-recent cave deposits, there are traces of occupancy by Neanderthal dating back to the late Pleistocene, 50 to 60 thousand years ago.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0abf05cb-0fda-4567-9d9d-0829911a8c66:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''In the sub-recent cave deposits, there are traces of occupancy by Neanderthal dating back to the late Pleistocene, 50 to 60 thousand years ago.'' restored on Paradise Cave', '81e27b74e23ae7b5379b757b789b93aca09869d7732b8d4291c1f5e8c3f802e8|d97eae48605020052d5d04131a59341c9f815e64d9d6b498b011fb4baeec180c|d97eae48605020052d5d04131a59341c9f815e64d9d6b498b011fb4baeec180c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1963 two students found Paradise Cave inside Malik hill, south of Kielce. Its cave deposits hold traces of Neanderthal occupancy from the late Pleistocene, 50 to 60 thousand years ago."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Paradise Cave was discovered in 1963 [S2]; it was discovered by two students [S2]; the cave lies inside Malik hill [S1]; it lies south of Kielce [S1]; its cave deposits hold traces of Neanderthal occupancy [S3]; the occupancy dates to the late Pleistocene [S3]; the occupancy dates to 50 to 60 thousand years ago [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Paradise Cave is a (limestone karst) cave in Poland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; The cave lies inside Malik hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; The cave lies south of Kielce [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; Paradise Cave was found in 1963 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; Paradise Cave was found by two students [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; The cave deposits hold traces of Neanderthal occupancy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; The Neanderthal traces date from the late Pleistocene [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]; The Neanderthal traces date to 50 to 60 thousand years ago [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paradise_Cave]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0b34fdec-f221-40f9-8238-2d78d7ce6638'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Southwest of Larissa, Cranon was a walled city of ancient Thessaly that minted silver coins, yet almost nothing is known of its acropolis, save a possible temple of Athena Polias.', 'Cranon or Crannon was a town and polis of Pelasgiotis, in ancient Thessaly, situated southwest of Larissa, and at the distance of 100 stadia from Gyrton, according to Strabo.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0b34fdec-f221-40f9-8238-2d78d7ce6638:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Cranon or Crannon was a town and polis of Pelasgiotis, in ancient Thessaly, situated southwest of Larissa, and at the distance of 100 stadia from Gyrton, according to Strabo.'' restored on Cranon', '575b42d3cbf1585e45c3e0b47f0edb7e2e986e254f7aed43feb27ba62e9a346e|988891e667c402c113732d22d43b8bf42140610200f1deaa6ace66026559fe0b|988891e667c402c113732d22d43b8bf42140610200f1deaa6ace66026559fe0b', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Southwest of Larissa, Cranon was a walled city of ancient Thessaly that minted silver coins, yet almost nothing is known of its acropolis, save a possible temple of Athena Polias."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cranon lay southwest of Larissa [S1]; Cranon was a walled city [S6]; Cranon was in ancient Thessaly [S1]; Cranon minted silver coins [S8]; almost nothing is known of its acropolis (a stated unknown) [S6]; the exception is a possible temple of Athena Polias on the acropolis [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cranon was an ancient city (polis) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; Cranon was in ancient Thessaly [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; Cranon lies southwest of Larissa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; Cranon was a walled city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; Cranon minted silver coins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; Almost nothing is known of Cranon''s acropolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]; A possible temple of Athena Polias stood on the acropolis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cranon]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0b681115-78b6-409f-b780-4330392e5b40'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The springs of the Roman Baths were dedicated to the goddess Sulis, perhaps at a Celtic centre of worship. A temple rose here between 60 and 70 AD; the baths grew over the next 300 years.', 'Roman baths built around 60–70 AD over natural hot springs sacred to the goddess Sulis. The water still flows at 46°C after nearly 2,000 years.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0b681115-78b6-409f-b780-4330392e5b40:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Roman baths built around 60–70 AD over natural hot springs sacred to the goddess Sulis. The water still flows at 46°C after nearly 2,000 years.'' restored on Roman Baths, Bath', '6b4095c4ad628d7c37013556a1752366d251be52bb46069b0203ca540ffe95e6|8746873672f22cf32d03c3641200dd0fd61dc899316a82595d82cb9bbc9b293a|8746873672f22cf32d03c3641200dd0fd61dc899316a82595d82cb9bbc9b293a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The springs of the Roman Baths were dedicated to the goddess Sulis, perhaps at a Celtic centre of worship. A temple rose here between 60 and 70 AD; the baths grew over the next 300 years."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Roman Baths have springs [S5, S6]; the springs were dedicated to the goddess Sulis [S6]; the site was perhaps a Celtic centre of worship (hedged) [S6]; a temple was built here [S2, S7]; the temple was built between 60 and 70 AD [S2, S7]; the baths were built up over the next 300 years [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Baths are an ancient Roman bath complex (thermae) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; The Roman Baths are in Bath, England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; The Roman Baths stand at natural springs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; The springs were dedicated to the goddess Sulis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; The site was perhaps a Celtic centre of worship [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; A temple was built on the site between 60 and 70 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]; The bathing complex grew over the following 300 years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Baths_(Bath)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0b994bf5-92ed-4592-a7f7-1a3d343cefd6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In Saumur, the Dolmen de Bagneux is the largest dolmen in France, its sandstone chamber stretching 17.3 metres. Likely Neolithic, circa 3000 BC, it would have received many burials.', 'Built around 3000 BC near Saumur, this dolmen is the tallest and most voluminous of its country''s 4,500 examples. No other dolmen in the region matches it in height or volume.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0b994bf5-92ed-4592-a7f7-1a3d343cefd6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "Built around 3000 BC near Saumur, this dolmen is the tallest and most voluminous of its country''s 4,500 examples. No other dolmen in the region matches it in height or volume." restored on Dolmen de Bagneux', '0a79e214e6dd5dbcaa239833ed5f06e0b00fb08f6b371ba2a53fc948523c3ccb|fa010b38db03eef266aaa0487b9bfc9fa90ce983cdbe1a36ae33a3dc91d0e7a2|fa010b38db03eef266aaa0487b9bfc9fa90ce983cdbe1a36ae33a3dc91d0e7a2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In Saumur, the Dolmen de Bagneux is the largest dolmen in France, its sandstone chamber stretching 17.3 metres. Likely Neolithic, circa 3000 BC, it would have received many burials."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Dolmen de Bagneux is in Saumur [S1]; it is the largest dolmen in France [S2]; it is built of sandstone [S5]; its chamber stretches 17.3 metres [S3]; it likely dates to the Neolithic [S4]; it likely dates to circa 3000 BC [S4]; it would have received many burials [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-005 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Dolmen de Bagneux is a megalithic dolmen in Saumur, France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; It is the largest dolmen in France [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; Its stones (chamber) are of sandstone [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; The chamber stretches 17.3 metres in length [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; It is likely Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; It dates to circa 3000 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]; It would have received many burials [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dolmen_de_Bagneux]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0cd2e708-f7f6-412d-91ba-6d00420b5056'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Locals call it Hetty Pegler''s Tump: Uley Long Barrow, a Neolithic burial mound probably built before 3000 BC. An excavation in 1821 revealed the remains of fifteen skeletons within.', 'A Neolithic gallery grave known locally as "Hetty Pegler''s Tump," built before 3000 BC. Its 37-meter mound hides a central passage with five stone chambers.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0cd2e708-f7f6-412d-91ba-6d00420b5056:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Neolithic gallery grave known locally as "Hetty Pegler\''s Tump," built before 3000 BC. Its 37-meter mound hides a central passage with five stone chambers.'' restored on Uley Long Barrow', 'a3d91eb1da27c72b6f467c6dd56fbfcdec07af92032b5625c57ba2d555fedaab|87b288364462d6e8c55986ca4046eaa2c88be5a285c6f980210ee6903608a1df|87b288364462d6e8c55986ca4046eaa2c88be5a285c6f980210ee6903608a1df', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Locals call it Hetty Pegler''s Tump: Uley Long Barrow, a Neolithic burial mound probably built before 3000 BC. An excavation in 1821 revealed the remains of fifteen skeletons within."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "locals call Uley Long Barrow Hetty Pegler''s Tump [S1]; it is a Neolithic burial mound [S1]; it was probably built before 3000 BC [S3]; it was excavated in 1821 [S7]; the excavation revealed the remains of fifteen skeletons within the barrow [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-006 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Uley Long Barrow is a Neolithic burial mound (a tomb) in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uley_Long_Barrow]; Locals call it Hetty Pegler''s Tump [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uley_Long_Barrow]; It was probably built before 3000 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uley_Long_Barrow]; It was excavated in 1821 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uley_Long_Barrow]; That excavation revealed the remains of fifteen skeletons within [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uley_Long_Barrow]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0d2486f1-4650-4c10-9f4c-b25a3e345012'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Nim Li Punit is Kekchi Maya for Big Hat, after the elaborate head-dress of an ancient king on a stela found here. Life around its three plazas ceased rather suddenly in the 9th century AD.', 'A Maya Classic Period site whose king once wore a headdress so elaborate it became the site''s modern name: "Big Hat." A jade pendant found here is among the finest Maya jewelry.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0d2486f1-4650-4c10-9f4c-b25a3e345012:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Maya Classic Period site whose king once wore a headdress so elaborate it became the site\''s modern name: "Big Hat." A jade pendant found here is among the finest Maya jewelry.'' restored on Nim Li Punit', '9bfff46c60d2e84bfae549016c5d52c58b9b62d6140bad22697e135d2668bd75|55045ae16d3ab48184cd4d2c2801787415c3d5fe081c6e39b4450955d9892d6d|55045ae16d3ab48184cd4d2c2801787415c3d5fe081c6e39b4450955d9892d6d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Nim Li Punit is Kekchi Maya for Big Hat, after the elaborate head-dress of an ancient king on a stela found here. Life around its three plazas ceased rather suddenly in the 9th century AD."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name Nim Li Punit is Kekchi Maya for Big Hat [S2]; the name refers to an elaborate head-dress [S2]; the head-dress belongs to an ancient king depicted on a stela [S2]; the stela was found at the site [S2]; the site has three plazas [S4]; occupation ceased rather suddenly [S6]; occupation ceased in the 9th century AD [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-006 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Nim Li Punit is an ancient Maya site (settlement) in Belize [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The name Nim Li Punit is Kekchi Maya [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The name means Big Hat [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The name refers to an elaborate head-dress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The head-dress is shown on a stela [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The stela was found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The head-dress belongs to an ancient king (of the site) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; The site has three plazas [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; Life (occupation) at the site ceased in the 9th century AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]; It ceased rather suddenly [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nim_Li_Punit]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0ea92399-96d7-4b98-839f-5ac77983e576'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Acropolis, 23 m east of the Parthenon, the Temple of Roma and Augustus rose circa 19 BCE: a marble ring of nine columns with no interior wall, a propaganda monument of Rome.', 'A circular Ionic temple built on the Acropolis around 19 BC, aligned with the Parthenon''s entrance. It was Roman propaganda in Athens'' most sacred space.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0ea92399-96d7-4b98-839f-5ac77983e576:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "A circular Ionic temple built on the Acropolis around 19 BC, aligned with the Parthenon''s entrance. It was Roman propaganda in Athens'' most sacred space." restored on Temple of Roma and Augustus', 'd2a7635558740866e84865df195b5fac642212c04874583b85ada1fcd67bb4f1|af9f74a01b04f3dc1e2ae808071822410713568d5d9f77c05bccc2d8862a9f7f|af9f74a01b04f3dc1e2ae808071822410713568d5d9f77c05bccc2d8862a9f7f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Acropolis, 23 m east of the Parthenon, the Temple of Roma and Augustus rose circa 19 BCE: a marble ring of nine columns with no interior wall, a propaganda monument of Rome."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Temple of Roma and Augustus stood on the Acropolis [S1]; it stood 23 m east of the Parthenon [S2]; it was built circa 19 BCE [S1]; it was made of marble [S4]; it was circular (a ring) [S1]; it had nine columns [S5]; it had no interior wall [S6]; it was a propaganda monument of Rome [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-006 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Temple of Roma and Augustus is a temple on the Acropolis (of Athens, Greece) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Roma_and_Augustus]; It stood 23 m east of the Parthenon [SUPPORTED: https://ancientathens3d.com/temple-rome-augustus/]; It was built circa 19 BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Roma_and_Augustus]; It was made of marble [SUPPORTED: https://ancientathens3d.com/temple-rome-augustus/]; It was a ring (circular building) of columns [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=8222]; It had nine columns [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=8222]; It had no interior wall [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=8222]; It was a propaganda monument of Rome [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Temple_of_Roma_and_Augustus]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0eec5ffa-1383-4b6b-92d1-cdc1f1dc0cfb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Inside Densuș Church, the oldest Orthodox stone church in Romania, 15th-century murals by the artist Ștefan show Jesus in traditional Romanian clothes. From 1566 it served Calvinists too.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0eec5ffa-1383-4b6b-92d1-cdc1f1dc0cfb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description None restored on Densuș Church', '47322201eacaea964b3655169131ed75d37d12c4247e9ecdf7687d42ee365b96|dc31f6d1f1fc706e9fd688255768f874d5514e3393f29680737d055175e7188a|dc31f6d1f1fc706e9fd688255768f874d5514e3393f29680737d055175e7188a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Inside Densuș Church, the oldest Orthodox stone church in Romania, 15th-century murals by the artist Ștefan show Jesus in traditional Romanian clothes. From 1566 it served Calvinists too."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Densuș Church is the oldest Orthodox stone church in Romania [S1]; there are murals inside the church [S5]; the murals date to the 15th century [S5]; the murals were made by the artist Ștefan [S6]; the murals show Jesus in traditional Romanian clothes [S5]; from 1566 the church also served Calvinists [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-006 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Densuș Church is an Orthodox stone church in Romania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99_Church]; It is the oldest Orthodox stone church in Romania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99]; Inside are 15th-century murals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99_Church]; The murals are by the artist Ștefan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99_Church]; The murals show Jesus in traditional Romanian clothes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99_Church]; From 1566 the church also served Calvinists [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Densu%C8%99_Church]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0f2a7448-cb04-4aee-90b5-deb59d72647f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Two ditches form the perimeter of Castle Dore, an Iron Age hill fort near Golant. Digging inside it from 1936, Ralegh Radford found postholes of huts from at least two phases of occupation.', 'Two ditches enclosing a circular area 79 metres in diameter near Fowey in Cornwall. Occupied from the 5th or 4th century BC, later linked by medieval romance to the legend of Tristan and Iseult.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0f2a7448-cb04-4aee-90b5-deb59d72647f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Two ditches enclosing a circular area 79 metres in diameter near Fowey in Cornwall. Occupied from the 5th or 4th century BC, later linked by medieval romance to the legend of Tristan and Iseult.'' restored on Castle Dore', '1d327e4a4b31c0a0ac7134e394f4c9721bbecaf6214082076bb143c7034e6ad6|693c3691329c5ed9964f4fc8b2ff313087997232a5c7d9bf97cbe99d58e99be3|693c3691329c5ed9964f4fc8b2ff313087997232a5c7d9bf97cbe99d58e99be3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-002", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Two ditches form the perimeter of Castle Dore, an Iron Age hill fort near Golant. Digging inside it from 1936, Ralegh Radford found postholes of huts from at least two phases of occupation."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-002 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the perimeter of Castle Dore consists of two ditches [S3]; Castle Dore is an Iron Age hill fort [S1]; it is near Golant [S1]; Ralegh Radford excavated there [S5]; the excavation began in 1936 [S5]; Radford dug the interior of the fort [S6]; he found postholes of huts [S6]; the huts belonged to at least two phases of occupation [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-006 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Castle Dore is a hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; It dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; It lies near Golant (Cornwall, England) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; It has two ditches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; The two ditches form its perimeter [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; Ralegh Radford excavated at Castle Dore [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; The digging began in 1936 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; Radford dug inside the fort (its interior) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; He found postholes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; The postholes belonged to huts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]; The huts came from at least two phases of occupation [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Castle_Dore]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0f785541-1f36-4dc8-9daa-3ebc5c540bd0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'South of the River Swale stood Cataractonium, a Roman fort where the road north from Eboracum split in two. Among its finds lay a theatrical mask, hinting at a possible theatre.', 'Among the artifacts excavated at the site was a theatrical mask, suggesting there may have been a theatre or small amphitheatre.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0f785541-1f36-4dc8-9daa-3ebc5c540bd0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Among the artifacts excavated at the site was a theatrical mask, suggesting there may have been a theatre or small amphitheatre.'' restored on Cataractonium', '9b37e4196e929dcc158c4e4bc2a4eefda7bae7b5ed6ec90eae2a570e066ce3a0|85515f479f57129b4fc3d138f6acdca6f5942d8f56170a4f4c0924d542da9344|85515f479f57129b4fc3d138f6acdca6f5942d8f56170a4f4c0924d542da9344', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "South of the River Swale stood Cataractonium, a Roman fort where the road north from Eboracum split in two. Among its finds lay a theatrical mask, hinting at a possible theatre."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cataractonium stood south of the River Swale [S7]; Cataractonium was a Roman fort [S1]; at Cataractonium the road north from Eboracum split in two [S6]; a theatrical mask was among its finds [S4]; the mask hints at a possible theatre [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-007 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Cataractonium was a Roman fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]; The fort stood south of the River Swale [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]; The road north from Eboracum reached a junction at Cataractonium [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]; At Cataractonium the road split in two (Dere Street and the Roman Watling Street) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]; A theatrical mask was among the finds from the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]; The mask hints at a possible theatre [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cataractonium]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('0f8924db-4ea5-4e63-88f5-866f06bb2248'::uuid, 'card_description', 'An 8 metre corridor led into the Anta do Monte Abraão, a Neolithic dolmen that excavations suggest was a tomb for about 80 individuals. Its finds include flint arrowheads and stone axes.', 'Excavations traced roughly 80 individuals buried here between 4000 and 2500 BC — part of a trio of megalithic tombs clustered together in what is now Queluz.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:0f8924db-4ea5-4e63-88f5-866f06bb2248:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Excavations traced roughly 80 individuals buried here between 4000 and 2500 BC — part of a trio of megalithic tombs clustered together in what is now Queluz.'' restored on Anta do Monte Abraão', '2cd4f115609c12423abb3d6f11909f1fb3ecb9710199e4bd10227bca18c9ade4|a291154907aecf925e76777977b7353144404bcdfb1c53310af940d84eff4b48|a291154907aecf925e76777977b7353144404bcdfb1c53310af940d84eff4b48', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "An 8 metre corridor led into the Anta do Monte Abraão, a Neolithic dolmen that excavations suggest was a tomb for about 80 individuals. Its finds include flint arrowheads and stone axes."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "an 8 metre corridor led into the dolmen [S6]; the Anta do Monte Abraão is a dolmen [S1]; it is Neolithic [S3]; excavations suggest it was a tomb for about 80 individuals [S3]; its finds include flint arrowheads [S7]; its finds include stone axes [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-007 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Anta do Monte Abraão is a dolmen (megalithic tomb) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]; The dolmen dates to the Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]; An 8 metre corridor led into it [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]; Excavations suggest it was a tomb for about 80 individuals [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]; Its finds include flint arrowheads [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]; Its finds include stone axes [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anta_do_Monte_Abra%C3%A3o]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1032047c-3a0c-413b-bd70-23b81f64487e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Across a ridge of Dartmoor, Shovel Down holds stone rows of probable Bronze Age date and the Fourfold Circle, four concentric stone circles where a turf-covered cairn keeps a robbed cist.', 'Dartmoor megaliths including stone rows, a fourfold stone circle, and standing stones from 3300–500 BC. Four concentric circles in one site is exceptionally rare.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1032047c-3a0c-413b-bd70-23b81f64487e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Dartmoor megaliths including stone rows, a fourfold stone circle, and standing stones from 3300–500 BC. Four concentric circles in one site is exceptionally rare.'' restored on Shovel Down', '7e37067c6fde7fd5c464f4f1eb091b7e5602538450309f7520bbed011d65132b|6df5a1a54a2d977f46bf76ba8e7f76d5e151334e41167752daf7940a8abcfa70|6df5a1a54a2d977f46bf76ba8e7f76d5e151334e41167752daf7940a8abcfa70', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Across a ridge of Dartmoor, Shovel Down holds stone rows of probable Bronze Age date and the Fourfold Circle, four concentric stone circles where a turf-covered cairn keeps a robbed cist."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shovel Down is on Dartmoor [S1]; it lies across a ridge [S2]; Shovel Down holds stone rows [S1, S4]; the stone rows are of probable Bronze Age date [S3]; Shovel Down holds the Fourfold Circle [S1]; the Fourfold Circle is four concentric stone circles [S5]; a turf-covered cairn lies at the Fourfold Circle [S6]; the cairn has a cist that has been robbed [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-007 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Shovel Down is a megalithic area [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; Shovel Down lies on Dartmoor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; Its monuments lie across a ridge [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; Shovel Down holds stone rows [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; The stone rows are of probable Bronze Age date [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; Shovel Down holds the Fourfold Circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; The Fourfold Circle is four concentric stone circles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; A turf-covered cairn lies at the Fourfold Circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]; The cairn has a cist that has been robbed [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shovel_Down]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('115be95f-85b1-472e-86b4-e68c8f55bdd5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At Vindolanda, just south of Hadrian''s Wall, Roman forts were built one on top of the other. Its wooden tablets were, when found, the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain.', 'A Roman fort predating Hadrian''s Wall, famous for the Vindolanda tablets -- thin wooden letters that are among the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:115be95f-85b1-472e-86b4-e68c8f55bdd5:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "A Roman fort predating Hadrian''s Wall, famous for the Vindolanda tablets -- thin wooden letters that are among the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain." restored on Vindolanda', 'ab8aeb452e47a8a3e2a01114f67e73b9b27a359f717c24df952cc6f225b140e0|93cef07fb64d86289601a380e982520935c90a899f3c13cd584c72ee85080a0e|93cef07fb64d86289601a380e982520935c90a899f3c13cd584c72ee85080a0e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At Vindolanda, just south of Hadrian''s Wall, Roman forts were built one on top of the other. Its wooden tablets were, when found, the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vindolanda lies just south of Hadrian''s Wall [S1]; Roman forts were built there one on top of the other [S1, S6]; Vindolanda has wooden tablets [S4]; when found, the tablets were the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-007 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vindolanda is a Roman fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindolanda]; Vindolanda lies just south of Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindolanda]; Roman forts at Vindolanda were built one on top of the other [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindolanda]; Vindolanda yielded wooden tablets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindolanda]; When found, the tablets were the oldest surviving handwritten documents in Britain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vindolanda]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('116de084-cd63-4ec0-aeca-4702b91b1bdb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Military Road now cuts through Hunnum, the fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, built between 122 and 126 AD. A slab from its west gate names the Legio VI Victrix as its first builders.', 'The fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, garrisoned by Roman cavalry. Coins, tombstones, sandals, and a spear point have surfaced from beneath the modern village.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:116de084-cd63-4ec0-aeca-4702b91b1bdb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "The fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, garrisoned by Roman cavalry. Coins, tombstones, sandals, and a spear point have surfaced from beneath the modern village." restored on Hunnum', 'ae94bf14abf841bac857c1b2a1f616bf3562070d6727bd46ef9f3439d9551721|7f408053af8a83757589da092268f44e0479da4e290ea4ca9a55c44b6062a925|7f408053af8a83757589da092268f44e0479da4e290ea4ca9a55c44b6062a925', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Military Road now cuts through Hunnum, the fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall, built between 122 and 126 AD. A slab from its west gate names the Legio VI Victrix as its first builders."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Military Road now cuts through Hunnum [S3]; Hunnum was the fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall [S2]; it was built between 122 and 126 AD [S4]; a slab from its west gate names the Legio VI Victrix [S5]; the Legio VI Victrix were its first builders [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-007 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hunnum is a Roman fort on Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]; The Military Road now cuts through the fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]; It was the fifth fort on Hadrian''s Wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]; It was built between 122 and 126 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]; A slab from its west gate names the Legio VI Victrix [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]; The Legio VI Victrix were its first builders [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunnum]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('120f7321-78f7-4786-a3a3-13d6b3c9d0f6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Kazanlak, a corridor 13 metres long leads into the three spacious rooms of the Tomb of Seuthes III, a temple of the 5th century BC where the Thracian king was buried around 300 BC.', 'Originally a 5th-century BC temple, this mound was repurposed around 300 BC to entomb Seuthes III, a Thracian king. His lifelike bronze portrait head survived intact.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:120f7321-78f7-4786-a3a3-13d6b3c9d0f6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Originally a 5th-century BC temple, this mound was repurposed around 300 BC to entomb Seuthes III, a Thracian king. His lifelike bronze portrait head survived intact.'' restored on Tomb of Seuthes III', 'f1dc15c080098c63cb8953289e43ff8d19bea6cc124749ab131c57efbf0d2365|61d7bcf71a6521be8bb9aa78c544ba06255533a3a4a11e52c80b2153aaf2ad1d|61d7bcf71a6521be8bb9aa78c544ba06255533a3a4a11e52c80b2153aaf2ad1d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Kazanlak, a corridor 13 metres long leads into the three spacious rooms of the Tomb of Seuthes III, a temple of the 5th century BC where the Thracian king was buried around 300 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Tomb of Seuthes III is near Kazanlak [S1]; a corridor 13 metres long leads into it [S3]; it has three spacious rooms [S3]; it was a temple of the 5th century BC [S4]; Seuthes III was a Thracian king [S2]; the king was buried there around 300 BC [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-008 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Tomb of Seuthes III is a tomb near Kazanlak (Bulgaria) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; A corridor 13 metres long leads into the tomb [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; The corridor leads into three spacious rooms [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; The structure was a temple [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; The temple dates to the 5th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; Seuthes III was a Thracian king [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]; Seuthes III was buried in it around 300 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomb_of_Seuthes_III]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('13b5bbe7-ff6b-4a1e-a8f5-d17ed3fee4f6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the sea lay Gnatia, a frontier town of the Messapii famed for a solar and fire cult that Pliny described and Horace ridiculed. It is famous for the discoveries made in its tombs.', 'A coastal city with Bronze Age origins from the 15th century BC, later the frontier town of the Messapii. Multiple invasions — Iapyges, Romans, Ostrogoths — reshaped it over millennia.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:13b5bbe7-ff6b-4a1e-a8f5-d17ed3fee4f6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A coastal city with Bronze Age origins from the 15th century BC, later the frontier town of the Messapii. Multiple invasions — Iapyges, Romans, Ostrogoths — reshaped it over millennia.'' restored on Gnatia', '10e992de8eb37c04b478621ff720fbd7e183394fe76dc9f52b752e7e0a503c93|64b53ee5a3e0861551b7eafcf495187e2376e49c965598c18bcbef93f3967240|64b53ee5a3e0861551b7eafcf495187e2376e49c965598c18bcbef93f3967240', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-003", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the sea lay Gnatia, a frontier town of the Messapii famed for a solar and fire cult that Pliny described and Horace ridiculed. It is famous for the discoveries made in its tombs."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-003 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gnatia lay on the sea [S4]; Gnatia was a frontier town of the Messapii [S1]; it was famed for a solar and fire cult [S5]; Pliny described the cult [S5]; Horace ridiculed the cult [S5]; it is famous for the discoveries made in its tombs [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-008 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Gnatia was an anci