    ('190fdea4-2036-47fa-a6ca-81b3b0ab2e93'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Where an open valley near Lagos begins to narrow lies the Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta: a straight wall of rectangular section, raised during the Romanization of Hispania.', 'Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta is an archeologic site of a Roman dam in the parish of São Sebastião, municipality of Lagos.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:190fdea4-2036-47fa-a6ca-81b3b0ab2e93:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta is an archeologic site of a Roman dam in the parish of São Sebastião, municipality of Lagos.'' restored on Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta', '4d0de04de9990a9ca360cc1e8eb90189eaac42b85752c66a77446856b5908cab|017241bf2961b993b9857496aee54183f4d7764cea5660a37eaef22e2eec1c87|017241bf2961b993b9857496aee54183f4d7764cea5660a37eaef22e2eec1c87', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Where an open valley near Lagos begins to narrow lies the Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta: a straight wall of rectangular section, raised during the Romanization of Hispania."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the dam lies in an open valley [S5]; it is built where the valley begins to narrow [S6]; it is near Lagos [S1]; it is a Roman dam [S1]; it is a straight wall of rectangular section [S4]; it was built during the Romanization of Hispania [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-001 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Dam of Fonte Coberta is an ancient Roman dam in Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam lies near Lagos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam lies in an open valley [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam stands where the valley begins to narrow [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam is a straight wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam wall has a rectangular section [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]; The dam was built during the Romanization of Hispania [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roman_Dam_of_Fonte_Coberta]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('197dd3af-e63a-48fc-80a1-ed694a5629fc'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Västerås rises Anundshög, the largest tumulus in Sweden. At its foot, 2 large stone ships lie end to end, one of them 51 metres long, on what was once a thing-place.', 'The largest tumulus in the region, from the 3rd century AD, surrounded by stone ship settings and runestones. Viking-era mounds like this held elite burials with grave goods.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:197dd3af-e63a-48fc-80a1-ed694a5629fc:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The largest tumulus in the region, from the 3rd century AD, surrounded by stone ship settings and runestones. Viking-era mounds like this held elite burials with grave goods.'' restored on Anundshög', '7370450a5a5df5f64608d6f0e3d1d833c7ac8339ebc1612ac84e25e8448aa27a|79c3239f2e292788e3aa08689e8348ecd8eaa650346c292868b181b734928432|79c3239f2e292788e3aa08689e8348ecd8eaa650346c292868b181b734928432', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Västerås rises Anundshög, the largest tumulus in Sweden. At its foot, 2 large stone ships lie end to end, one of them 51 metres long, on what was once a thing-place."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Anundshög is near Västerås [S1]; Anundshög is a tumulus [S1]; it is the largest tumulus in Sweden [S1]; 2 large stone ships lie at its foot [S5]; the stone ships are placed end to end [S5]; one of the stone ships is 51 metres long [S5]; the site was once a thing-place [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-001 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Anundshög is a tumulus (burial mound) in Sweden [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; Anundshög lies near Västerås [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; Anundshög is the largest tumulus in Sweden [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; At the foot of the mound lie 2 large stone ships [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; The two stone ships lie end to end [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; One of the stone ships is 51 metres long [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]; The site was once a thing-place [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anundsh%C3%B6g]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('19f1118c-190f-47f7-a161-8d6cd52277e7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Two holes left by a collapse of undetermined date open the roof of Vela Spila Cave. Here lay 36 ceramic artifacts, the only Upper Palaeolithic ceramic figurative art in southeastern Europe.', 'Occupied from 21,000 BC, this elliptical cave on Korcula island is 40 m long and 17 m high. Oldest known habitation site in the eastern Adriatic, with two natural openings in the cavern roof.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:19f1118c-190f-47f7-a161-8d6cd52277e7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Occupied from 21,000 BC, this elliptical cave on Korcula island is 40 m long and 17 m high. Oldest known habitation site in the eastern Adriatic, with two natural openings in the cavern roof.'' restored on Vela Spila Cave', '6c6ccb48053fc72cb632d93504b7a352bb43e8434ed84be1ccee684bf7d449d4|002c537fe8633d1ee1d18d4f93bee57652d13516c537c1dbfaff2c18d0b958a3|002c537fe8633d1ee1d18d4f93bee57652d13516c537c1dbfaff2c18d0b958a3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Two holes left by a collapse of undetermined date open the roof of Vela Spila Cave. Here lay 36 ceramic artifacts, the only Upper Palaeolithic ceramic figurative art in southeastern Europe."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "there are two openings (holes) in the roof of the cave [S3]; the openings were caused by a collapse [S3]; the date of the collapse is undetermined (unknown) [S3]; 36 ceramic artifacts were found in the cave [S7]; these finds are the only Upper Palaeolithic ceramic figurative art in southeastern Europe [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-010 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Vela Spila is a cave with archaeological deposits in Croatia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vela_Spila]; Two holes (openings) open the roof of the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vela_Spila]; The two roof openings were left by a collapse [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vela_Spila]; The date of the collapse is undetermined [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vela_Spila]; 36 ceramic artifacts were found in the cave [SUPPORTED: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0041437]; The ceramic artifacts date to the Upper Palaeolithic [SUPPORTED: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0041437]; They are the only Upper Palaeolithic ceramic figurative art in southeastern Europe [SUPPORTED: https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0041437]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1a26d6f1-d77a-4bb4-a018-afb806a4cd15'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 1966, 300 meters from the Playa de El Rinconcillo in Algeciras, the Roman Kilns of El Rinconcillo came to light: two furnaces joined as one, vaults resting on a column and eight arches.', 'Roman kiln complex discovered in 1966 just 300 metres from a beach in Algeciras. Part of Portus Albus, it supplied containers to the processing industries of the neighbouring city of Iulia Traducta.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1a26d6f1-d77a-4bb4-a018-afb806a4cd15:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Roman kiln complex discovered in 1966 just 300 metres from a beach in Algeciras. Part of Portus Albus, it supplied containers to the processing industries of the neighbouring city of Iulia Traducta.'' restored on Roman Kilns of El Rinconcillo, Algeciras', 'ef29fe4c21c09f1941e49f636af5d14e1908058329bcd5a17cdad3b424a66b49|37b0d2116f917e747f44985cb87cbafa1c92b399bd7ece007d8014e52fc81b32|37b0d2116f917e747f44985cb87cbafa1c92b399bd7ece007d8014e52fc81b32', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 1966, 300 meters from the Playa de El Rinconcillo in Algeciras, the Roman Kilns of El Rinconcillo came to light: two furnaces joined as one, vaults resting on a column and eight arches."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the kilns were discovered in 1966 [S2]; they lie 300 meters from the Playa de El Rinconcillo [S2]; they are in Algeciras [S1]; they are Roman kilns [S1]; they consist of two furnaces joined into a single unit [S3]; the vaults rest on a (central) column and eight arches [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-010 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Roman Kilns of El Rinconcillo are Roman pottery kilns in Algeciras, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; The kilns came to light (were discovered) in 1966 [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; They lie 300 metres from the Playa de El Rinconcillo [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; The site consists of two furnaces (kilns) [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; The two furnaces are joined as one unit [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; Their vaults rest on a (central) column [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]; The vaults rest on eight arches [SUPPORTED: https://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hornos_romanos_de_El_Rinconcillo]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1a9242d4-250f-4ffb-8dad-dae849c10798'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Chichen Itza, Balankanche Cave has drawn rituals to the Maya rain god Chaac for more than two thousand years. Inside were found stairs, altars and ritual displays of ceramics.', 'A Maya cave near Chichén Itzá sealed for centuries with offerings to the rain god Tlaloc still arranged around a massive stalagmite. The ritual assemblage was found untouched.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1a9242d4-250f-4ffb-8dad-dae849c10798:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Maya cave near Chichén Itzá sealed for centuries with offerings to the rain god Tlaloc still arranged around a massive stalagmite. The ritual assemblage was found untouched.'' restored on Balankanche Cave', '2419918d2f8b667add77212b8b80b6711d9af324e45cc1ecaf99787719c31c79|96a958075efd712c8c3728cf0830175c23644e29421c79c1559af1fcd0e31eb6|96a958075efd712c8c3728cf0830175c23644e29421c79c1559af1fcd0e31eb6', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Chichen Itza, Balankanche Cave has drawn rituals to the Maya rain god Chaac for more than two thousand years. Inside were found stairs, altars and ritual displays of ceramics."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Balankanche Cave lies near Chichen Itza [S1]; the cave has been the focus of rituals to the Maya rain god Chaac [S2]; these rituals span more than two thousand years [S2]; stairs were found inside the cave [S4]; altars were found inside the cave [S4]; ritual displays of ceramics were found inside the cave [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-010 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Balankanche is a Maya ritual cave near Chichen Itza [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balankanche]; The cave has been the focus of rituals to the Maya rain god Chaac [SUPPORTED: https://lugares.inah.gob.mx/es/node/4372]; These rituals have gone on for more than two thousand years [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balankanche]; Stairs were found inside the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balankanche]; Altars were found inside the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balankanche]; Ritual displays of ceramics were found inside the cave [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Balankanche]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1b82a32d-cf01-4735-94c2-7df578796ce5'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Kherlen River basin, the Khitan built Bars-Hot, a city 1.6 by 1.8 kilometres ringed with mud walls. Near them a ruined octagonal brick pagoda of the 11th century has lost its top.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1b82a32d-cf01-4735-94c2-7df578796ce5:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description None restored on Bars-Hot', 'e737995e1d4aa41716031c2ce3e807e23060b25255294d2549675567fb2d3e75|5b39ba827d7b438b540fcafd916aa63bb0b01df1272dfa085d2da598c628f248|5b39ba827d7b438b540fcafd916aa63bb0b01df1272dfa085d2da598c628f248', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Kherlen River basin, the Khitan built Bars-Hot, a city 1.6 by 1.8 kilometres ringed with mud walls. Near them a ruined octagonal brick pagoda of the 11th century has lost its top."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bars-Hot lies in the Kherlen River basin [S1]; Bars-Hot was built by the Khitan [S1]; Bars-Hot was a city [S1]; the city measured 1.6 by 1.8 kilometres [S3]; the city was surrounded by mud walls [S3]; a pagoda stands near the city walls [S4]; the pagoda is ruined, octagonal and built of brick [S4]; the pagoda dates to the 11th century [S4]; the pagoda''s top is missing [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-010 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Bars-Hot is a (walled) city in Mongolia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The city lies in the Kherlen River basin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The city was built by the Khitan [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The city measures 1.6 by 1.8 kilometres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The city is ringed with mud walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; A pagoda stands near the city walls [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The pagoda is ruined [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The pagoda is octagonal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The pagoda is built of brick [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The pagoda dates to the 11th century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]; The pagoda has lost its top [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bars-Hot]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1bc57ad9-041a-42de-b0a1-c361a7beb531'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Picture ancient Greek athletes competing in the stadion: the Stadium at Nemea, in Corinthia, was built 400 m southeast of the Temple of Zeus on the ancient site of Nemea.', 'Athletes competed in foot races just metres from the Temple of Zeus. A rectangular starting block—the ancient equivalent of a starting line—was found on site.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1bc57ad9-041a-42de-b0a1-c361a7beb531:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Athletes competed in foot races just metres from the Temple of Zeus. A rectangular starting block—the ancient equivalent of a starting line—was found on site.'' restored on Stadium at Nemea', '54ea43e24ec42d454e1868bcf6f7ab9fd720baff02c82dbc7c1628f61b6cc44a|e4337b95dda5340162da1fc7ec4c82d8e996a4b8904d77af7dcbbc62cbeb0b69|e4337b95dda5340162da1fc7ec4c82d8e996a4b8904d77af7dcbbc62cbeb0b69', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Picture ancient Greek athletes competing in the stadion: the Stadium at Nemea, in Corinthia, was built 400 m southeast of the Temple of Zeus on the ancient site of Nemea."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "ancient Greek athletes competed in the stadion here [S2]; the Stadium at Nemea is in Corinthia [S1]; it is on the ancient site of Nemea [S1]; it was built 400 m southeast of the Temple of Zeus [W1]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-001 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Stadium at Nemea is an ancient Greek stadium in Greece [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=1815]; The stadium lies on the ancient site of Nemea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_at_Nemea]; The stadium is in Corinthia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_at_Nemea]; Ancient Greek athletes competed in the stadion (foot race) at the stadium [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stadium_at_Nemea]; The stadium was built 400 m from the Temple of Zeus [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=1815]; The stadium lies southeast of the Temple of Zeus [SUPPORTED: http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/2/eh251.jsp?obj_id=1815]"}, {"source": "web fact W1", "url": "http://odysseus.culture.gr/h/3/eh3530.jsp?obj_id=2511", "quote": "The Stadium, which could accomodate 40.000 spectators, was built 400 m SE of the Temple of Zeus."}]'::jsonb),
    ('1c18de52-3783-41cb-a3dd-ade50498a974'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In the Wiehen Hills, 255.6 metres above sea level, the Babilonie hillfort of the La Tène culture encloses over 12 hectares, where ceramics of the Saxon-Frankish period have also turned up.', 'A Celtic hillfort from 300-150 BC at an elevation that commanded views across the surrounding landscape. La Tène culture forts like this served as trade hubs and tribal centers.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1c18de52-3783-41cb-a3dd-ade50498a974:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Celtic hillfort from 300-150 BC at an elevation that commanded views across the surrounding landscape. La Tène culture forts like this served as trade hubs and tribal centers.'' restored on Babilonie', '927d6fad398600f5c4f16f28e80288e5710952e22a068671dcd4c7a8017d3ac0|43524d0722f4680a6c5668fde7551f3bf68131da340a08f350d41094977ade05|43524d0722f4680a6c5668fde7551f3bf68131da340a08f350d41094977ade05', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In the Wiehen Hills, 255.6 metres above sea level, the Babilonie hillfort of the La Tène culture encloses over 12 hectares, where ceramics of the Saxon-Frankish period have also turned up."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Babilonie is in the Wiehen Hills [S1]; it lies 255.6 metres above sea level [S1]; Babilonie is a hillfort [S1]; it belongs to the La Tène culture [S1]; the fortification encloses over 12 hectares [S2]; ceramics of the Saxon-Frankish period have also been found there [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-011 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Babilonie is a hillfort in Germany. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babilonie]; It lies in the Wiehen Hills. [SUPPORTED: https://www.altertumskommission.lwl.org/de/forschung/burgen/die-wallburg-babilonie/]; It lies at 255.6 metres above sea level. [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babilonie]; The hillfort belongs to the La Tène culture. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babilonie]; The hillfort encloses over 12 hectares. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babilonie]; Ceramics of the Saxon-Frankish period have also been found there. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Babilonie]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1c899f53-4414-4954-821d-9119802aa39a'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At the mouth of the Lao River lay Laüs, where Herodotus says survivors of Sybaris, destroyed in 510 BC, took refuge. A tomb found by accident held red-figured vases and bronze armour.', 'Herodotus states that the inhabitants of Sybaris who had survived the destruction of their city in 510 BC took refuge in Laus and Scidrus.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1c899f53-4414-4954-821d-9119802aa39a:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Herodotus states that the inhabitants of Sybaris who had survived the destruction of their city in 510 BC took refuge in Laus and Scidrus.'' restored on Laüs', 'eda27eb807ec2b2aef0519d9b303fa60dd38aab9114736df08d93313b81c26f1|a1c4a3a29c503d03019c706e55ca1ba675dd4cbae12a04cbb08f49c205fde656|a1c4a3a29c503d03019c706e55ca1ba675dd4cbae12a04cbb08f49c205fde656', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At the mouth of the Lao River lay Laüs, where Herodotus says survivors of Sybaris, destroyed in 510 BC, took refuge. A tomb found by accident held red-figured vases and bronze armour."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Laüs lay at the mouth of the Lao River [S2]; Herodotus says survivors of Sybaris took refuge in Laüs [S4]; Sybaris was destroyed in 510 BC [S4]; a tomb was found by accident [S6]; the tomb held red-figured vases [S6]; the tomb held bronze armour [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-001 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Laüs was an ancient city whose site lies in Calabria, Italy [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]; Laüs lay at the mouth of the Lao River [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]; Herodotus says survivors of Sybaris took refuge in Laüs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]; Sybaris was destroyed in 510 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris]; A tomb at Laüs was found by accident [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]; The tomb held red-figured vases [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]; The tomb held bronze armour [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/La%C3%BCs]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1d7c880b-80be-41b1-8be5-58d07a0e5dcd'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Remote even today, Naachtun was named by Sylvanus Morley after a Mayan phrase glossed as distant stones. Its ancient name was Masuul, and 45 stelae have been excavated among its ruins.', 'By the Terminal Classic, Naachtun suffered a population loss leaving many of the residential structures abandoned.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1d7c880b-80be-41b1-8be5-58d07a0e5dcd:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''By the Terminal Classic, Naachtun suffered a population loss leaving many of the residential structures abandoned.'' restored on Naachtun', '0a45f2425b54c6ed5fd98c84deaa8a17eb75dcafb33d85e68ae5ea3f9c824b3f|a39fb42b21a42e61f1060ff07b28150d5988728d222db5414f443eff83b36e6f|a39fb42b21a42e61f1060ff07b28150d5988728d222db5414f443eff83b36e6f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Remote even today, Naachtun was named by Sylvanus Morley after a Mayan phrase glossed as distant stones. Its ancient name was Masuul, and 45 stelae have been excavated among its ruins."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Naachtun is remote even today (far from contemporary settlements) [S2]; Sylvanus Morley named the site Naachtun [S3]; the name comes from a Mayan construction glossed as ''distant stones'' [S3]; the name refers to its remoteness [S3]; its ancient name was Masuul [S4]; 45 stelae have been excavated at Naachtun [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-011 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Naachtun is a ruined ancient Maya city (archaeological site) in Guatemala. [SUPPORTED: https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/archaeology/maya_naachtun_01.shtml]; Naachtun is remote even today. [SUPPORTED: https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/archaeology/maya_naachtun_01.shtml]; The site was named by Sylvanus Morley. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naachtun]; The name comes from a Mayan phrase glossed as ''distant stones''. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naachtun]; Its ancient name was Masuul. [SUPPORTED: https://www.bbc.co.uk/history/ancient/archaeology/maya_naachtun_01.shtml]; 45 stelae have been found (excavated) among its ruins. [SUPPORTED: https://www.persee.fr/doc/crai_0065-0536_2016_num_160_3_95999]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1d873464-bd56-415a-984e-67026294d00c'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a small headland of North Ronaldsay, the Iron Age Broch of Burrian gave up painted pebbles, part of an iron bell of Celtic type and a cross-slab with an ogham inscription.', 'The Broch of Burrian is an Iron Age broch located on North Ronaldsay in the Orkney Islands. The Broch of Burrian was excavated by William Traill, proprietor of the island, in 1870 and 1871.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1d873464-bd56-415a-984e-67026294d00c:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The Broch of Burrian is an Iron Age broch located on North Ronaldsay in the Orkney Islands. The Broch of Burrian was excavated by William Traill, proprietor of the island, in 1870 and 1871.'' restored on Broch of Burrian', '546fd824c4e7367b23145e2eec3ca40aa067e9a8d1c2ea746269e6a27edf2351|a106c19b9191c566547f887a5081acef3104c8253b3b06342cf42c23943f5489|a106c19b9191c566547f887a5081acef3104c8253b3b06342cf42c23943f5489', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a small headland of North Ronaldsay, the Iron Age Broch of Burrian gave up painted pebbles, part of an iron bell of Celtic type and a cross-slab with an ogham inscription."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the broch stands on a small headland [S2]; it is on North Ronaldsay [S1]; it is an Iron Age broch [S1]; painted pebbles were found there [S8]; part of an iron bell of Celtic type was found there [S8]; a cross-slab with an ogham inscription was found there [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-011 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Broch of Burrian is a broch on North Ronaldsay, Scotland. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Burrian]; The broch dates to the Iron Age. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Burrian]; It stands on a small headland. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Burrian]; Painted pebbles were found at the broch. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Burrian]; Part of an iron bell of Celtic type was found at the broch. [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broch_of_Burrian]; A cross-slab with an ogham inscription was found at the broch. [SUPPORTED: https://ogham.celt.dias.ie/S-ORK-001]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1e7ec452-34c4-4630-ac4e-e53d0dcbd184'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Takht-i-Bahi lies Seri Bahlol, on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1980, its relics associated with Buddha not yet properly excavated. Statues and coins are commonly found.', 'The ruins of Seri Bahlol are the remnants of a small ancient fortified town built during the Kushan period.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1e7ec452-34c4-4630-ac4e-e53d0dcbd184:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The ruins of Seri Bahlol are the remnants of a small ancient fortified town built during the Kushan period.'' restored on Seri Bahlol', '96b4801ebafcc684ff7c789e1e36f5d9533dc88645e133b1d40314e1524c3fdc|033adaac3d728883561daf6ad513ffaf7fe1fbb2872751c555084a53b17af167|033adaac3d728883561daf6ad513ffaf7fe1fbb2872751c555084a53b17af167', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Takht-i-Bahi lies Seri Bahlol, on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1980, its relics associated with Buddha not yet properly excavated. Statues and coins are commonly found."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Seri Bahlol lies near Takht-i-Bahi [S1]; it has been on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1980 [S2]; it contains relics associated with Buddha [S5]; those relics have not been properly excavated [S5]; statues are commonly found there [S6]; coins are commonly found there [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Seri Bahlol is an archaeological site (the remains of a small ancient fortified town) located near Takht-i-Bahi [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]; Seri Bahlol has been on the UNESCO World Heritage List since 1980 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]; The site contains relics associated with the Buddha [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]; These relics associated with the Buddha have not yet been properly excavated [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]; Statues are commonly found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]; Coins are commonly found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seri_Bahlol]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1ed00cd4-fec4-406f-af91-8c6bf79eeb3d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In southern Corsica, the menhirs of Filitosa were raised around 1500 BC, 2-3 metres tall and carved with human faces, armour and weapons. Arrow heads and pottery trace life here to 3300 BC.', 'Megalithic standing stones from 3300 BC carved with human faces, swords, and armor — rare stone portraits produced at the dawn of the Bronze Age in the western Mediterranean.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1ed00cd4-fec4-406f-af91-8c6bf79eeb3d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Megalithic standing stones from 3300 BC carved with human faces, swords, and armor — rare stone portraits produced at the dawn of the Bronze Age in the western Mediterranean.'' restored on Filitosa', 'e4f6fcbaf5b5a7b0b322da276cedc1b11dce92c6b4c03666ec00c8d5adb96314|b2578201967c32273d975624c26cabbfafc3ad29379fd3aa3cea1ac153b865a3|b2578201967c32273d975624c26cabbfafc3ad29379fd3aa3cea1ac153b865a3', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In southern Corsica, the menhirs of Filitosa were raised around 1500 BC, 2-3 metres tall and carved with human faces, armour and weapons. Arrow heads and pottery trace life here to 3300 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Filitosa is in southern Corsica [S1]; Filitosa has menhirs [S6]; the menhirs were erected around 1500 BC [S6]; the menhirs are 2-3 metres tall [S6]; the menhirs are carved with human faces, armour and weapons [S7]; finds of arrow heads and pottery date the earliest inhabitation to 3300 BC [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-012 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Filitosa is a megalithic site with menhirs in southern Corsica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; The menhirs were raised around 1500 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; The menhirs are 2-3 metres tall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; The menhirs are carved with human faces [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; The menhirs are carved with armour [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; The menhirs are carved with weapons [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; Arrow heads and pottery were found at the site [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]; These finds trace life at the site back to 3300 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Filitosa]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1ef25b31-5c42-4ee6-aae1-1bd9c2640bff'::uuid, 'card_description', NULL, 'In January 2011, the earliest known winery in the world was uncovered in the cave.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1ef25b31-5c42-4ee6-aae1-1bd9c2640bff:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''In January 2011, the earliest known winery in the world was uncovered in the cave.'' restored on Areni-1 Cave', '||abea90530c870eafc5e434d5316ac1e7db0a38bca95d7db8d58ccb482ea1d6ce', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: contradicted-after-verify", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The web check contradicts: The shoe from the cave is the earliest known shoe in the world (https://www.worldhistory.org/Areni_Cave/: \"While not the oldest shoe in the world, it is the world''s oldest leather shoe.\"); The web check contradicts: The brain from the cave is the oldest humanoid brain (https://www.earth.ox.ac.uk/article/ancient-human-brains: \"Many of these brains were up to 12,000 years old, and found in records dating back to the mid-17th century.\"); The web check contradicts: The winery was uncovered in January 2011 (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Areni-1_winery: \"Excavations at the Areni-1 site, located in a three-chambered karstic cave along the Arpa River, began in 2007 and concluded in September 2010.\")"}]'::jsonb),
    ('1f020df7-29e0-4b61-aecc-80e294e7b59d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Occupied for more than 2,500 years, the Maya city of Izamal was built of megalithic carved blocks with rounded corners, later a site of pilgrimage rivaled in the region only by Chichen Itza.', 'Called "The City of Hills," its five massive Mayan pyramids were long mistaken for natural hills by locals. Human settlement here began around 550 AD.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1f020df7-29e0-4b61-aecc-80e294e7b59d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Called "The City of Hills," its five massive Mayan pyramids were long mistaken for natural hills by locals. Human settlement here began around 550 AD.'' restored on Izamal', '7873d3087fc39ca3503398602131b23e91ca24e849e974ac9db1f01c98a79c51|75ce81ee948f10adb3fcbbd658207c58c907cb46d5eec90c67c707e158770b2d|75ce81ee948f10adb3fcbbd658207c58c907cb46d5eec90c67c707e158770b2d', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Occupied for more than 2,500 years, the Maya city of Izamal was built of megalithic carved blocks with rounded corners, later a site of pilgrimage rivaled in the region only by Chichen Itza."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Izamal was occupied for more than 2,500 years [W1]; Izamal is a Maya city [S1, S2]; it was built with megalithic carved blocks [S3]; its construction featured rounded corners [S3]; it later became a site of pilgrimage [S6]; as a pilgrimage site it was rivaled in the region only by Chichen Itza [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Izamal is a Maya city (an archaeological site of the Pre-Columbian Maya civilization) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izamal]; Izamal was occupied for more than 2,500 years [SUPPORTED: https://lugares.inah.gob.mx/en/node/4422]; Izamal was built of megalithic carved blocks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izamal]; Izamal''s buildings have rounded corners [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izamal]; Izamal later became a site of pilgrimage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izamal]; As a pilgrimage site Izamal was rivaled in the region only by Chichen Itza [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Izamal]"}, {"source": "web fact W1", "url": "https://lugares.inah.gob.mx/en/node/4422", "quote": "The site was occupied for a long period, in excess of 2,500 years, since it began in the Middle Preclassic period (700-450 BC)."}]'::jsonb),
    ('1fd1292d-5dbf-4bbf-a501-6a0e094da785'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Peneus near Larissa lay Argura, the Argissa of Homer''s Iliad. Its walls with square towers date to the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, and dedications honour Apollo Pythius and Artemis.', 'Homer named it Argissa in the Iliad — the 7th-century BC town of Argura on the Peneus near Larissa. Ancient scholars debated whether it was simply a neighbourhood of the larger city.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:1fd1292d-5dbf-4bbf-a501-6a0e094da785:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Homer named it Argissa in the Iliad — the 7th-century BC town of Argura on the Peneus near Larissa. Ancient scholars debated whether it was simply a neighbourhood of the larger city.'' restored on Argura', '077496a70233e45d05b386169fecfd7f7ae2d7a8636e3a85d6b46f2a422dd0a0|c2ff138b726a82fd7ec4767c6e20ba2f275e433888df176c20fd40095f6ac2b2|c2ff138b726a82fd7ec4767c6e20ba2f275e433888df176c20fd40095f6ac2b2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-004", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Peneus near Larissa lay Argura, the Argissa of Homer''s Iliad. Its walls with square towers date to the 5th and 4th centuries BCE, and dedications honour Apollo Pythius and Artemis."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-004 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Argura lay on the Peneus [S1]; Argura lay near Larissa [S1]; Argura was a town (it ''lay'' there) [S1]; Argura is the Argissa of Homer''s Iliad [S1]; the site has walls with square towers [S4]; the walls date to the 5th and 4th centuries BCE [S4]; dedications found there honour (attest the cult of) Apollo Pythius and Artemis [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-012 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Argura was an ancient town (polis) in Thessaly [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Argura lay on the Peneus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Argura lay near Larissa [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Argura is the Argissa of Homer''s Iliad [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Its walls have square towers [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; The walls date to the 5th and 4th centuries BCE [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Dedications honour Apollo Pythius [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]; Dedications honour Artemis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Argura]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('20a18387-7871-456c-967a-c94abd69e3e0'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Seuthopolis, seat of the Thracian king Seuthes III and his court, was founded between 325–315 BC. In its cemetery, brick tholos tombs held the upper class, sometimes with their horses.', 'An ancient Thracian capital founded 325–315 BC, now entirely submerged beneath a reservoir. It can only be studied when water levels drop.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:20a18387-7871-456c-967a-c94abd69e3e0:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''An ancient Thracian capital founded 325–315 BC, now entirely submerged beneath a reservoir. It can only be studied when water levels drop.'' restored on Seuthopolis', '3ad82bb2ee5012fb0e218f3bda6a019e95e1d5ddc32a2fa217778eccf86cc248|93bd4298dcf5a72b6922a3bf29de43a234b6d69d448dd0d533185882ea9a4597|93bd4298dcf5a72b6922a3bf29de43a234b6d69d448dd0d533185882ea9a4597', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Seuthopolis, seat of the Thracian king Seuthes III and his court, was founded between 325–315 BC. In its cemetery, brick tholos tombs held the upper class, sometimes with their horses."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Seuthopolis was the seat of Seuthes III and his court [S2]; Seuthes III was a Thracian king [S1]; Seuthopolis was founded between 325 and 315 BC [S1]; Seuthopolis had a cemetery with brick tholos tombs [S3]; the upper class were interred in these tombs [S3]; the upper class were sometimes buried with their horses [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Seuthopolis (Bulgaria) was the seat of the Thracian king Seuthes III and his court, an ancient royal city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]; Seuthes III was a Thracian king [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]; Seuthopolis was founded between 325 and 315 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]; Seuthopolis had a cemetery with brick tholos tombs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]; The brick tholos tombs held the upper class [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]; The upper class were sometimes buried with their horses [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seuthopolis]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('21660e33-c3b7-462f-aaec-cbaded7b4a8d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Armazi, original capital of the early Georgian kingdom of Kartli, had ready access to the Daryal Pass, the main road over the Greater Caucasus. The Arab invasion of the 730s destroyed it.', 'Armazi''s strategic situation was dictated by its ready access to the Daryal Pass, the main road over the Greater Caucasus.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:21660e33-c3b7-462f-aaec-cbaded7b4a8d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "Armazi''s strategic situation was dictated by its ready access to the Daryal Pass, the main road over the Greater Caucasus." restored on Armazi', '1147fb17a5e5a45c42518a1e6cd55e3caf1aefac74387b7853403eca70ba6a9d|8048d4eba2c0807721923aeebbdb0c3f715d33c7554b272e542ac9d83c375d13|8048d4eba2c0807721923aeebbdb0c3f715d33c7554b272e542ac9d83c375d13', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Armazi, original capital of the early Georgian kingdom of Kartli, had ready access to the Daryal Pass, the main road over the Greater Caucasus. The Arab invasion of the 730s destroyed it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Armazi was the original capital of the early Georgian kingdom of Kartli [S2]; Armazi had ready access to the Daryal Pass [S7]; the Daryal Pass is the main road over the Greater Caucasus [S7]; Armazi was destroyed by the Arab invasion [S3]; the Arab invasion took place in the 730s [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Armazi (Georgia) was the original capital of the early Georgian kingdom of Kartli [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]; Kartli was an early Georgian kingdom [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]; Armazi had ready access to the Daryal Pass [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]; The Daryal Pass is the main road over the Greater Caucasus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]; Armazi was destroyed by an Arab invasion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]; The destruction took place in the 730s [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Armazi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('21a181ef-2027-4c76-a8a4-3e59a0b1e51b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Souvloto Mouri, a hilltop in eastern Crete with no wells or springs, Chamaizi holds the only known oval-shaped Minoan building, centred on a cistern that likely caught the rain.', 'The only known oval-shaped building from the Minoan period, built around a central cistern. Below it lie even earlier foundations from the Early Minoan era.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:21a181ef-2027-4c76-a8a4-3e59a0b1e51b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The only known oval-shaped building from the Minoan period, built around a central cistern. Below it lie even earlier foundations from the Early Minoan era.'' restored on Chamaizi', '5cb1264ee7a2be9b800f2532f86ea2ae7932425b9aa23d4a18742b5401808f32|15126f67af5352a4079843d6049c86b46f1fba05000a3819d867bc0534cbbc3c|15126f67af5352a4079843d6049c86b46f1fba05000a3819d867bc0534cbbc3c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Souvloto Mouri, a hilltop in eastern Crete with no wells or springs, Chamaizi holds the only known oval-shaped Minoan building, centred on a cistern that likely caught the rain."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Souvloto Mouri is a hilltop [S5]; the site lies in eastern Crete [S1]; the hilltop has no wells or springs [S5]; the building stands on Souvloto Mouri [S5]; Chamaizi has the only known oval-shaped building of the Minoan period [S1]; the building is centred on a cistern [S4]; the cistern likely collected rainwater (hedged) [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Chamaizi is a Minoan archaeological site with a building on the hilltop Souvloto Mouri in eastern Crete [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]; Souvloto Mouri is the hilltop on which the building stands [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]; The site is in eastern Crete [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]; The hilltop has no wells or springs [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]; The building at Chamaizi is the only known oval-shaped Minoan building [SUPPORTED: http://www.minoancrete.com/chamaizi.htm]; The building is centred on a cistern [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]; The cistern likely caught rainwater [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamaizi]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('21f3da5f-262c-46e8-bead-32be438da097'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the border of Mesoamerica lies Los Naranjos Archaeological Park. In 1935 Frans Blom and Jens Yde excavated a large mound here and found a large collection of polychrome pottery.', '350 BC-250 AD. On the north shore of Lake Yojoa, Los Naranjos holds the oldest structures still standing in the region - mounds, pyramids, and ceramics built by the Proto-Lenca over 2,000 years.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:21f3da5f-262c-46e8-bead-32be438da097:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''350 BC-250 AD. On the north shore of Lake Yojoa, Los Naranjos holds the oldest structures still standing in the region - mounds, pyramids, and ceramics built by the Proto-Lenca over 2,000 years.'' restored on Los Naranjos Archaeological Park', '1fc84f0d3099de9d3bedc8effc4e1a3eed6eac70757cc4e871fb56dc0e1ded5e|972ce5c3b385a76e08b4f0190f2d4e95174e83a551419fafc1247477033934a5|972ce5c3b385a76e08b4f0190f2d4e95174e83a551419fafc1247477033934a5', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the border of Mesoamerica lies Los Naranjos Archaeological Park. In 1935 Frans Blom and Jens Yde excavated a large mound here and found a large collection of polychrome pottery."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Los Naranjos lies on the border of Mesoamerica [S1]; in 1935 Frans Blom and Jens Yde excavated a large mound at the site [S3]; they found a large collection of polychrome pottery [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Los Naranjos Archaeological Park is an archaeological site on the border of Mesoamerica [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Naranjos,_Honduras]; Frans Blom and Jens Yde excavated at Los Naranjos in 1935 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Naranjos,_Honduras]; They excavated a large mound there [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Naranjos,_Honduras]; They found a large collection of polychrome pottery [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Los_Naranjos,_Honduras]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('22415177-5658-4973-ab15-ddad22663cde'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Archaeologists estimate over 130 million adobe bricks went into the Huaca del Sol, a Moche pyramid. In the early 17th century, colonists turned the Moche River past its base to loot gold.', 'By 450 AD, eight different stages of construction had been completed on the Huaca del Sol.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:22415177-5658-4973-ab15-ddad22663cde:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''By 450 AD, eight different stages of construction had been completed on the Huaca del Sol.'' restored on Huaca del Sol', '87eba563ea55c6898923ac4a3db0a1a3fa05127d8382028e1e55f715c7dafe2e|1b0af71148c6f86756196547c0ab315de4b5854d2af909647db68ebf5a34ebbc|1b0af71148c6f86756196547c0ab315de4b5854d2af909647db68ebf5a34ebbc', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Archaeologists estimate over 130 million adobe bricks went into the Huaca del Sol, a Moche pyramid. In the early 17th century, colonists turned the Moche River past its base to loot gold."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "archaeologists estimate the Huaca del Sol used over 130 million adobe bricks [S3]; the Huaca del Sol is a pyramid built by the Moche [S1]; in the early 17th century colonists redirected the Moche River past its base [S5]; the river was redirected to loot gold [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-013 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Huaca del Sol (Peru) is a Moche pyramid built of adobe bricks [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huaca_del_Sol]; Archaeologists estimate that over 130 million adobe bricks went into the Huaca del Sol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huaca_del_Sol]; In the early 17th century colonists diverted the Moche River to run past the base of the Huaca del Sol [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huaca_del_Sol]; The river was diverted to loot gold [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Huaca_del_Sol]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('23ee2849-5bee-4742-91b1-29a35361fcf7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Badgers were damaging Barrow Clump, an early Bronze Age bowl barrow in Wiltshire. Digging uncovered many Saxon graves and a bucket-shaped drinking vessel of yew wood bound with bronze.', 'The barrow was constructed in the early Bronze Age on the site of a Neolithic settlement, and much later, around the 6th century, it was re-used as an Anglo-Saxon cemetery.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:23ee2849-5bee-4742-91b1-29a35361fcf7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The barrow was constructed in the early Bronze Age on the site of a Neolithic settlement, and much later, around the 6th century, it was re-used as an Anglo-Saxon cemetery.'' restored on Barrow Clump', 'a8e02303d0c3154a2dcf97320114212c402ab23091b0480865a7eccb687ab863|6507f9c9999e737947721e22e8bced6f32899148a6876c6364350eeaf5938ace|6507f9c9999e737947721e22e8bced6f32899148a6876c6364350eeaf5938ace', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Badgers were damaging Barrow Clump, an early Bronze Age bowl barrow in Wiltshire. Digging uncovered many Saxon graves and a bucket-shaped drinking vessel of yew wood bound with bronze."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barrow Clump was being damaged by badgers [S6]; Barrow Clump is a bowl barrow [S3]; the barrow was built in the early Bronze Age [S4]; Barrow Clump is in Wiltshire [S1]; excavation uncovered many Saxon graves [S5, S6, S8]; excavation uncovered a bucket-shaped drinking vessel [S8]; the vessel is made of yew wood bound with bronze [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-014 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barrow Clump is a bowl barrow (a burial mound) in Wiltshire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; Barrow Clump is in Wiltshire [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; The barrow dates to the early Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; Badgers were damaging Barrow Clump [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; The site was dug (excavated) because of that damage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; The digging uncovered many Saxon graves [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; A bucket-shaped drinking vessel was found [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]; The vessel is made of yew wood bound with bronze [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barrow_Clump]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('24f1c052-5bc8-490e-9b1e-ccce0c05611b'::uuid, 'card_description', 'In 627, Bede tells, Bishop Paulinus accompanied King Edwin and Queen Æthelburg to their royal vill at Yeavering, on a terrace below the Iron Age hillfort that crowned Yeavering Bell.', '7th-century AD royal seat of Bernicia, built over Mesolithic remains. Its Anglo-Saxon timber halls were among the grandest in early medieval Northumbria - a power centre recorded in Bede''s writings.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:24f1c052-5bc8-490e-9b1e-ccce0c05611b:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "7th-century AD royal seat of Bernicia, built over Mesolithic remains. Its Anglo-Saxon timber halls were among the grandest in early medieval Northumbria - a power centre recorded in Bede''s writings." restored on Yeavering', 'e1f2aaa3c1a356841c1af2bef44eee1eb671a011f3c25592dc56b5bdd1e07ac5|046e350e002688d167578aa63fe9cba8426b426463413c6c6e7ca3700209a8bf|046e350e002688d167578aa63fe9cba8426b426463413c6c6e7ca3700209a8bf', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "In 627, Bede tells, Bishop Paulinus accompanied King Edwin and Queen Æthelburg to their royal vill at Yeavering, on a terrace below the Iron Age hillfort that crowned Yeavering Bell."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the event is told by Bede [S3]; in 627 Bishop Paulinus accompanied King Edwin and Queen Aethelburg [S3]; they went to their royal vill [S3]; the royal vill (Adgefrin / ad gefrin) was at Yeavering [S3, S4, S6]; the Anglo-Saxon settlement lay on a terrace below Yeavering Bell [S5]; an Iron Age hillfort stood on Yeavering Bell [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-014 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Yeavering was a royal vill (an Anglo-Saxon royal settlement) of King Edwin [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]; The royal vill lay on a terrace below Yeavering Bell [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]; Bede tells of the visit [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]; The visit took place in 627 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]; Bishop Paulinus accompanied King Edwin and Queen Æthelburg to Yeavering [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]; A hillfort crowned Yeavering Bell [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering_Bell]; The hillfort on Yeavering Bell is of the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yeavering]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('251201f8-0dae-473d-916d-4a7e5c1a970f'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Atop a steep hill, the Dacian Fortress of Căpâlna guarded the way from the Sebeș Valley to the Dacian capital, Sarmizegetusa Regia. Around 106 AD the Romans burnt and destroyed it.', 'A Dacian fortress guarding the approach to the capital Sarmizegetusa Regia. An observatory tower controlled the only valley entrance to the kingdom''s heart.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:251201f8-0dae-473d-916d-4a7e5c1a970f:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "A Dacian fortress guarding the approach to the capital Sarmizegetusa Regia. An observatory tower controlled the only valley entrance to the kingdom''s heart." restored on Dacian Fortress of Căpâlna', '6a2beb7e025e0e38993d8535a30dbf3b4af26816c2af6c88222d0a0434cc5680|e92098615821ed8dbd67a7c039cc0af39cdd4d3c326367ff42d5fc4ce339a414|e92098615821ed8dbd67a7c039cc0af39cdd4d3c326367ff42d5fc4ce339a414', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Atop a steep hill, the Dacian Fortress of Căpâlna guarded the way from the Sebeș Valley to the Dacian capital, Sarmizegetusa Regia. Around 106 AD the Romans burnt and destroyed it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the fortress stands at the top of a steep hill [S1]; it guarded the entrance from the Sebes Valley to Sarmizegetusa Regia [S1]; Sarmizegetusa Regia was the capital of the Dacian kingdom [S1]; the Romans burnt and destroyed it around 106 AD [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-014 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Dacian Fortress of Căpâlna is a Dacian fortress (a military stronghold) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dacian_fortress_of_C%C4%83p%C3%A2lna]; The fortress stands atop a steep hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dacian_fortress_of_C%C4%83p%C3%A2lna]; It guarded the way from the Sebeș Valley to Sarmizegetusa Regia [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dacian_fortress_of_C%C4%83p%C3%A2lna]; Sarmizegetusa Regia was the Dacian capital [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarmizegetusa_Regia]; The Romans burnt and destroyed the fortress [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dacian_fortress_of_C%C4%83p%C3%A2lna]; The destruction happened around 106 AD [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dacian_fortress_of_C%C4%83p%C3%A2lna]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('251ac618-a557-4c31-9129-65afc47b649d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Fleeing Thurii, where fellow colonists killed most of them, Sybarites founded Sybaris on the Traeis, by the river now called the Trionto. Diodorus Siculus writes the Bruttii destroyed it.', 'Sybaris on the Traeis was an ancient city situated on the Traeis river, now known as the Trionto.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:251ac618-a557-4c31-9129-65afc47b649d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Sybaris on the Traeis was an ancient city situated on the Traeis river, now known as the Trionto.'' restored on Sybaris on the Traeis', '1e178400cd37c99999647ec3833884c5248d3fda14ab286abdf58fc8b839ba04|adab3a44c359bbfcf90ac1631c116793c2a5de60e5bd29e43b8655f9ada6ed3c|adab3a44c359bbfcf90ac1631c116793c2a5de60e5bd29e43b8655f9ada6ed3c', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Fleeing Thurii, where fellow colonists killed most of them, Sybarites founded Sybaris on the Traeis, by the river now called the Trionto. Diodorus Siculus writes the Bruttii destroyed it."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "most of the Sybarites were killed by the other colonists of Thurii [S5]; Sybarites fled Thurii [S6]; the fleeing Sybarites founded Sybaris on the Traeis [S6]; the city lay on the river now called the Trionto [S1]; Diodorus Siculus writes that the Bruttii destroyed it [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-014 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Sybaris on the Traeis was an ancient city by the river Traeis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris_on_the_Traeis]; The river Traeis is now called the Trionto [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris_on_the_Traeis]; At Thurii, fellow colonists killed most of the Sybarites [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris_on_the_Traeis]; Sybarites fleeing Thurii founded Sybaris on the Traeis [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris_on_the_Traeis]; Diodorus Siculus writes that the Bruttii destroyed the city [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sybaris_on_the_Traeis]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('257ce429-7d0c-4444-8ed5-57d129c25d50'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Schanzenkopf, a wooded hill in the Spessart on the Hesse border, lie the remains of a circular rampart up to 12 m wide and 2 to 4 m high, generally attributed to the Hallstatt period.', 'A circular rampart fortification from 1200–450 BC on a wooded hilltop at the Hesse-Bavaria border. Such ring forts controlled strategic forest passes.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:257ce429-7d0c-4444-8ed5-57d129c25d50:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A circular rampart fortification from 1200–450 BC on a wooded hilltop at the Hesse-Bavaria border. Such ring forts controlled strategic forest passes.'' restored on Schanzenkopf - Schwedenschanze', '114a27ef3b0b888185acddb8bcf96028e0d68d64d4f3dc02748e18a9bf67ca92|4e8ff4e15a7a2dfc37f93faaafc77122001be6aafd101afe7cda355904faffbe|4e8ff4e15a7a2dfc37f93faaafc77122001be6aafd101afe7cda355904faffbe', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Schanzenkopf, a wooded hill in the Spessart on the Hesse border, lie the remains of a circular rampart up to 12 m wide and 2 to 4 m high, generally attributed to the Hallstatt period."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Schanzenkopf is a wooded hill [S1]; it is in the Spessart [S2]; it lies on the border with Hesse [S2]; the remains of a circular rampart lie on the hill [S3]; the rampart reaches a width of 12 m [S4]; the rampart is 2 to 4 m high [S4]; the rampart is generally attributed to the Hallstatt period [S5]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-014 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The remains of a circular rampart (a ring fort) lie on Schanzenkopf [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; Schanzenkopf is a wooded hill [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; Schanzenkopf is in the Spessart [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; Schanzenkopf lies on the Hesse border [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; The rampart is up to 12 m wide [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; The rampart is 2 to 4 m high [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; The rampart is generally attributed to the Hallstatt period [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]; The Hallstatt dating is an attribution, not a proven date [SUPPORTED: https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Schanzenkopf_(Schwedenschanze)]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('262b6a28-de86-4e63-bdba-398a67236618'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On Dartmoor, Brisworthy Stone Circle keeps 24 broad, low stones in a slight oval, probably only half the original number. A trial dig is said to have yielded charcoal and one flint flake.', '24 broad upright stones form a 24-metre oval on Dartmoor -- roughly half the original number. Located 300 metres from Ringmoor Stone Row and Cairn Circle in the Upper Plym Valley.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:262b6a28-de86-4e63-bdba-398a67236618:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''24 broad upright stones form a 24-metre oval on Dartmoor -- roughly half the original number. Located 300 metres from Ringmoor Stone Row and Cairn Circle in the Upper Plym Valley.'' restored on Brisworthy Stone Circle', '21afc08feae4d486c1c12ca1417254dc0d216dd5a485cab26b37e77f4ea8695c|80aace928e3381ca9e53527a7b8bef5e5a773becef61df85d75529cd844bf4d9|80aace928e3381ca9e53527a7b8bef5e5a773becef61df85d75529cd844bf4d9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On Dartmoor, Brisworthy Stone Circle keeps 24 broad, low stones in a slight oval, probably only half the original number. A trial dig is said to have yielded charcoal and one flint flake."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Brisworthy Stone Circle is on Dartmoor [S1]; the circle has 24 stones [S5]; the stones are broad [S6]; the stones are low (under 1 metre) [S6]; the circle is a slight oval [S4]; the 24 stones are probably only half the original number (hedged) [S5]; a trial excavation is said to have yielded charcoal [S8]; a trial excavation is said to have yielded one flint flake [S8]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Brisworthy Stone Circle is a stone circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; It lies on Dartmoor [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The circle has 24 stones [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The stones are broad [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The stones are low [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The stones stand in a slight oval [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The 24 stones are probably only half the original number [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; A trial dig was made at the circle [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The trial dig is said to have yielded charcoal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]; The trial dig is said to have yielded one flint flake [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brisworthy_stone_circle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('264ac74e-f5bc-4b73-b28a-c2db99132c50'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Wednesbury, Woden''s borough, is named after the pre-Christian god Woden. A large ditch dug up in 2008 is read as part of a hilltop enclosure, possibly the long suspected Iron Age hillfort.', 'A market town where a large Iron Age ditch was found in 2008, confirming a long-suspected hilltop enclosure. Anglo-Saxon battles were fought here centuries later.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:264ac74e-f5bc-4b73-b28a-c2db99132c50:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A market town where a large Iron Age ditch was found in 2008, confirming a long-suspected hilltop enclosure. Anglo-Saxon battles were fought here centuries later.'' restored on Wednesbury', 'b64c6d6b87d66ca5c902d31ed9b4a297a1a62b7368d985550a6a0f4728d5aebf|00b6cc471a029d24fba5119eab3c00585df81648cdb656d12800bf853cc1870a|00b6cc471a029d24fba5119eab3c00585df81648cdb656d12800bf853cc1870a', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-rewrite-v-001", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wednesbury, Woden''s borough, is named after the pre-Christian god Woden. A large ditch dug up in 2008 is read as part of a hilltop enclosure, possibly the long suspected Iron Age hillfort."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-v-001 (check-v), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wednesbury means Woden''s borough [S4]; Wednesbury is named after the pre-Christian god Woden [S4]; a large ditch was excavated in 2008 [S2]; the ditch is interpreted as part of a hilltop enclosure [S2]; the ditch is possibly part of the long suspected Iron Age hillfort [S2]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify2-002 (verify2), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Wednesbury is a town in England [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]; The name Wednesbury means Woden''s borough [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]; Wednesbury is named after the pre-Christian god Woden [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]; A large ditch was excavated (dug up) in 2008 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]; The ditch is interpreted as part of a hilltop enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]; The ditch is possibly part of the long-suspected Iron Age hillfort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wednesbury]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2655cac3-4a76-4ea5-bc1e-9922ce8c08c7'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Once in the wall of an Inca palace, the perfectly cut, finely finished diorite of the Twelve-Angled Stone now sits in the Hatun Rumiyoc, the outer wall of the Archbishop''s palace in Cusco.', 'A single diorite block with 12 precisely fitted angles, set into an Inca palace wall without mortar. It demonstrates the extreme precision of Inca stonework.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:2655cac3-4a76-4ea5-bc1e-9922ce8c08c7:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A single diorite block with 12 precisely fitted angles, set into an Inca palace wall without mortar. It demonstrates the extreme precision of Inca stonework.'' restored on Twelve-Angled Stone', '061f99d06d3aec304215cd6ee73361e7f73b8e3dd2035a74dbbb8d5035e36ecb|76d275c20a8164a98922c0de0a9de9b074a8e8c70d20e5095f36e812e8bf43c9|76d275c20a8164a98922c0de0a9de9b074a8e8c70d20e5095f36e812e8bf43c9', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Once in the wall of an Inca palace, the perfectly cut, finely finished diorite of the Twelve-Angled Stone now sits in the Hatun Rumiyoc, the outer wall of the Archbishop''s palace in Cusco."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the stone was part of a wall of an Inca palace [S2]; the stone is perfectly cut [S5]; the stone is finely finished [S4]; the stone is of diorite [S4]; the stone is now part of the Hatun Rumiyoc wall [S5]; the Hatun Rumiyoc forms the outside of the Archbishop''s palace [S5]; the Archbishop''s palace is in Cusco [S1, S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Twelve-Angled Stone is a cut stone block set in a wall in Cusco, Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone was once part of the wall of an Inca palace [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone is perfectly cut [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone is finely finished [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone has twelve angles [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone is diorite [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The stone now sits in the Hatun Rumiyoc wall [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]; The Hatun Rumiyoc is the outer wall of the Archbishop''s palace in Cusco [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twelve-angled_stone]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('26c567ca-9af0-486b-9599-dcb0b7792628'::uuid, 'card_description', 'The Anakeion was the Athens temple of Castor and Pollux, twin sons of Zeus. Pausanias says Mikon depicted the Argonauts there, and Polygnotos the twins seizing the daughters of Leukippos.', '5th-century BC Athenian sanctuary of Castor and Pollux. In 415 and 411 BC, citizens mustered here before military campaigns — making it as much a war ground as a sacred precinct.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:26c567ca-9af0-486b-9599-dcb0b7792628:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''5th-century BC Athenian sanctuary of Castor and Pollux. In 415 and 411 BC, citizens mustered here before military campaigns — making it as much a war ground as a sacred precinct.'' restored on Anakeion', 'f933edf86d831d07a234e5d73569c838618e9fcfcc8216ec675547682e0932d2|0ef2446825544a9769cc618e47679de3b7afddb2ecee46d864dd52b3c02297b2|0ef2446825544a9769cc618e47679de3b7afddb2ecee46d864dd52b3c02297b2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Anakeion was the Athens temple of Castor and Pollux, twin sons of Zeus. Pausanias says Mikon depicted the Argonauts there, and Polygnotos the twins seizing the daughters of Leukippos."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the Anakeion was a temple in Athens [S1]; the temple was dedicated to Castor and Pollux (the Dioskouroi) [S1, S2]; Castor and Pollux were twin sons of Zeus [S2]; according to Pausanias, Mikon decorated the temple [S4]; Mikon depicted the Argonauts [S5]; according to Pausanias, Polygnotos decorated the temple [S4]; Polygnotos depicted the Dioskouroi abducting the daughters of Leukippos [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "The Anakeion was a temple of Castor and Pollux [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; The Anakeion was in Athens [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Castor and Pollux were twins [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Castor and Pollux were sons of Zeus [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Pausanias is the source for the paintings of Mikon and Polygnotos in the Anakeion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Mikon painted in the Anakeion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Mikon depicted the Argonauts [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Pausanias says Mikon painted the Argonauts in the sanctuary of the Dioscuri [SUPPORTED: https://www.theoi.com/Text/Pausanias1B.html]; Polygnotos painted in the Anakeion [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]; Polygnotos depicted the twins seizing the daughters of Leukippos [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anakeion]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('27d651ad-32dc-4bbc-bbb9-9218d9aec357'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Near Black Dog in Devon, Berry Castle, an earthwork probably of the Iron Age, breaks the hill fort pattern: a seemingly incomplete enclosure on the slope of a hill that peaks at 199 metres.', 'Iron Age earthwork in Devon sitting on a hillslope rather than a hilltop — defying the usual hill fort pattern. Its incomplete enclosure shape has never been fully explained by archaeologists.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:27d651ad-32dc-4bbc-bbb9-9218d9aec357:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''Iron Age earthwork in Devon sitting on a hillslope rather than a hilltop — defying the usual hill fort pattern. Its incomplete enclosure shape has never been fully explained by archaeologists.'' restored on Berry Castle, Black Dog', '77e8532d887ad0004c9636c66516bdbf5e8c29ace480bef3d6f1c7717cef6371|32a95576a73bc0a241ba39cdada150e608e1fecb56f8f139b2ba2f93afe01cb2|32a95576a73bc0a241ba39cdada150e608e1fecb56f8f139b2ba2f93afe01cb2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-005", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Near Black Dog in Devon, Berry Castle, an earthwork probably of the Iron Age, breaks the hill fort pattern: a seemingly incomplete enclosure on the slope of a hill that peaks at 199 metres."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-005 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Berry Castle is near Black Dog in Devon [S1]; Berry Castle is an earthwork [S1]; it probably dates to the Iron Age (hedged) [S1]; it does not fit the traditional hill fort pattern [S2]; it would seem to be an incomplete enclosure (hedged) [S3]; it lies on the slope of a hill rather than its top [S3]; the hill peaks at 199 metres [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-015 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Berry Castle is an earthwork [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; It lies near Black Dog [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; It lies in Devon [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; The earthwork probably dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; It breaks the hill fort pattern [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; It is seemingly an incomplete enclosure [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; It lies on the slope of a hill, not at its top [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]; The hill peaks at 199 metres [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Berry_Castle,_Black_Dog]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('280f2b7d-4978-4352-896d-bcbb03837d3e'::uuid, 'card_description', 'At the lake Pumaqucha, in the Vischongo District of Ayacucho, lies Inti Watana, an archaeological site that Peru declared a National Cultural Heritage on July 27, 2001.', NULL, 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:280f2b7d-4978-4352-896d-bcbb03837d3e:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description None restored on Inti Watana, Ayacucho', 'af532b37a23a87c1151d268cc5b2bb5985756e916ae3eea4b3472c788582e8a8|632bbf0cfbd89fff81c37b08ae433d616f77b264706c3a2bd35b1b49cdfb0629|632bbf0cfbd89fff81c37b08ae433d616f77b264706c3a2bd35b1b49cdfb0629', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "At the lake Pumaqucha, in the Vischongo District of Ayacucho, lies Inti Watana, an archaeological site that Peru declared a National Cultural Heritage on July 27, 2001."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site lies at the lake Pumaqucha [S2]; it is in the Vischongo District [S2]; it is in Ayacucho (the Ayacucho Region) [S2]; the site is called Inti Watana [S1]; Inti Watana is an archaeological site [S1]; Peru declared it a National Cultural Heritage [S3]; the declaration was made on July 27, 2001 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Inti Watana is an archaeological site at the lake Pumaqucha in the Vischongo District of Ayacucho, Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; The site lies at the lake Pumaqucha [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; The site is in the Vischongo District [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; The site is in Ayacucho [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; The site is in Peru [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; Peru declared the site a National Cultural Heritage [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]; The declaration was made on July 27, 2001 [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inti_Watana,_Ayacucho]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2817ce5e-3b43-445e-9a10-d4115a7036bb'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On the Ebro''s west bank, Late Bronze Age cottages grew into the First Iron Age streets of Barranc de Gàfols: at least 17 households, a fireplace in each, and one building with stone mills.', 'A Bronze and Iron Age site with continuous occupation, featuring rectangular stone houses from the First Iron Age. Imported Phoenician trade goods show Mediterranean contacts.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:2817ce5e-3b43-445e-9a10-d4115a7036bb:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Bronze and Iron Age site with continuous occupation, featuring rectangular stone houses from the First Iron Age. Imported Phoenician trade goods show Mediterranean contacts.'' restored on Barranc de Gàfols', 'f5fc7743d0a0b954c40cb6cfe979ab0e193c71b75f70913f63171b9b43a5ad61|fed987260ea2fecb949d561d4a359e42ab21facd9bbed81b41c2faa47dd458e4|fed987260ea2fecb949d561d4a359e42ab21facd9bbed81b41c2faa47dd458e4', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On the Ebro''s west bank, Late Bronze Age cottages grew into the First Iron Age streets of Barranc de Gàfols: at least 17 households, a fireplace in each, and one building with stone mills."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the site is on the west bank of the Ebro [S3]; cottages were established there in the Late Bronze Age [S4]; occupation continued from the Late Bronze Age into the First Iron Age [S2]; in the First Iron Age the settlement had streets [S5]; the First Iron Age settlement had at least 17 households [S5]; a fireplace was found in each household [S6]; one building held stone mills [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Barranc de Gàfols is a Late Bronze Age and First Iron Age settlement site on the west bank of the Ebro [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; The site lies on the west bank of the Ebro [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; Cottages stood at the site in the Late Bronze Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; The Late Bronze Age settlement grew into the First Iron Age settlement (continuous occupation) [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; The First Iron Age settlement had streets [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; There were at least 17 households [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; A fireplace was found in each household [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]; One building had stone mills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Barranc_de_G%C3%A0fols]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('285feaf7-cf87-443a-bfeb-a8e7607a1e91'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Behind Ashdown Park lies Alfred''s Castle, a small Iron Age hill fort begun in the 6th century BC amid late Bronze Age ditches. In the late 1st century a Romano-British farmhouse rose inside.', 'Started in the 6th century BC within a series of late Bronze Age linear ditches, this small enclosure''s attached cropmark field — invisible at ground level — only appeared in aerial survey.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:285feaf7-cf87-443a-bfeb-a8e7607a1e91:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description "Started in the 6th century BC within a series of late Bronze Age linear ditches, this small enclosure''s attached cropmark field — invisible at ground level — only appeared in aerial survey." restored on Alfred''s Castle', '96763abaf6950de72e0a5f286985ad9745e5b634289e9dcc1431de0cabf8c4c2|7b76c7443136662c90f103446ebaad65582b4e19a7c74a1d6e1fe9e05c0b9a97|7b76c7443136662c90f103446ebaad65582b4e19a7c74a1d6e1fe9e05c0b9a97', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Behind Ashdown Park lies Alfred''s Castle, a small Iron Age hill fort begun in the 6th century BC amid late Bronze Age ditches. In the late 1st century a Romano-British farmhouse rose inside."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alfred''s Castle lies behind Ashdown Park [S1]; it is a small Iron Age hill fort [S1]; the hill fort (small enclosure) was started in the 6th century BC [S4]; it was set within late Bronze Age ditches [S5]; in the late 1st century a Romano-British farmhouse was built inside the enclosure [S6]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Alfred''s Castle is a small Iron Age hill fort behind Ashdown Park [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; It lies behind Ashdown Park [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; The hill fort is small [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; The hill fort dates to the Iron Age [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; The hill fort was begun in the 6th century BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; The hill fort was set amid late Bronze Age ditches [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; A Romano-British farmhouse was built inside the hill fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]; The farmhouse was built in the late 1st century [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alfred%27s_Castle]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('28b1639a-d35e-4fef-bfac-78dbb00a1d69'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Over a slate gorge of the Erges, Segura Bridge links Portugal to Spanish Alcántara. Only the arches by the banks are Roman, yet the 1571 stonework is deemed barely distinguishable from them.', 'A Roman bridge connecting two modern countries, built over the Erges river in the 1st century AD. Only two of its five arches are original Roman stonework.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:28b1639a-d35e-4fef-bfac-78dbb00a1d69:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Roman bridge connecting two modern countries, built over the Erges river in the 1st century AD. Only two of its five arches are original Roman stonework.'' restored on Segura Bridge', 'f60d3c26fb56f00583a8d6f4bf6a18999d1164fb2347ae755c6c912c6b9c49e0|6c31fc1186e5ed071603e93971c4e57b024e94a13a471525df5430490633625f|6c31fc1186e5ed071603e93971c4e57b024e94a13a471525df5430490633625f', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Over a slate gorge of the Erges, Segura Bridge links Portugal to Spanish Alcántara. Only the arches by the banks are Roman, yet the 1571 stonework is deemed barely distinguishable from them."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the bridge spans a gorge in the local slate [S5]; the river crossed is the Erges (the gorge''s river, Erjas) [S2, S5]; Segura Bridge links Portugal to the Spanish municipality of Alcantara [S1]; only the arches nearest the river banks are original Roman arches [S3]; the 1571 reconstruction stonework is considered barely distinguishable from the original [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Segura Bridge is a (Roman) bridge over the Erges linking Portugal to Alcántara in Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The bridge crosses the Erges river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The bridge spans a gorge of the river [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The gorge is cut in slate [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The bridge''s Portuguese end is in Portugal [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The bridge''s other end is in Alcántara, Spain [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; Only the arches next to the river banks are Roman [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; Stonework on the bridge dates from a 1571 reconstruction [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]; The 1571 stonework is considered barely distinguishable from the Roman work [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Segura_Bridge]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('29446177-8f7d-4e23-9a9c-6c9ba2959472'::uuid, 'card_description', 'South of the Cuillin hills on Skye, on an uninhabited headland whose name means point of the little fort, lies Rubha an Dùnain Passage Grave, amid sites from the Mesolithic onwards.', 'A 3rd-millennium BC passage grave on a headland below the Cuillin hills. Designated a nationally important historic monument in 2017.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:29446177-8f7d-4e23-9a9c-6c9ba2959472:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A 3rd-millennium BC passage grave on a headland below the Cuillin hills. Designated a nationally important historic monument in 2017.'' restored on Rubha an Dùnain Passage Grave', '43bb600908b9dc899b037a77ef92bfde9281ee03d788e68de650ba01884bdc18|490a76b6a9d655a8e1d8ba275f520d959544d694aca5190e786fb1a0d786e2c2|490a76b6a9d655a8e1d8ba275f520d959544d694aca5190e786fb1a0d786e2c2', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "South of the Cuillin hills on Skye, on an uninhabited headland whose name means point of the little fort, lies Rubha an Dùnain Passage Grave, amid sites from the Mesolithic onwards."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the headland is south of the Cuillin hills [S1]; the headland is on Skye [S1]; the headland is uninhabited [S1]; it is a headland [S3]; its name means point of the little fort [S5]; Rubha an Dùnain Passage Grave lies on this Rubha an Dùnain headland, among its archaeological sites [S1, S4]; the headland holds sites from the Mesolithic onwards [S4]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-016 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Rubha an Dùnain Passage Grave is a passage grave (prehistoric chambered tomb) on the Rubha an Dùnain headland of Skye [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; The headland is on Skye [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; The headland lies south of the Cuillin hills [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; The headland is uninhabited [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; Rubha an Dùnain is a headland [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; The headland''s name means point of the little fort [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]; The passage grave lies amid archaeological sites dating from the Mesolithic onwards [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rubha_an_D%C3%B9nain]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('294968f3-036b-44ff-b704-91593648927d'::uuid, 'card_description', 'On a rocky promontory in northern Kea, Kephala was a Final Neolithic settlement of small rectangular rooms, home perhaps to 45-80 people, recently dated to about 4600-4500 BC.', 'A Late Neolithic open settlement from the 5th millennium BC -- the only significant unfortified settlement of its period in the Cyclades.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:294968f3-036b-44ff-b704-91593648927d:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''A Late Neolithic open settlement from the 5th millennium BC -- the only significant unfortified settlement of its period in the Cyclades.'' restored on Kephala, Kea', '8a9b7667d5a12612c78823e06efd53cd383660893f083dce1501088137ebd5c3|bb91896f3832fd248f8b7a6c369d226b30564f4265bb17600418551031b8c24e|bb91896f3832fd248f8b7a6c369d226b30564f4265bb17600418551031b8c24e', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "On a rocky promontory in northern Kea, Kephala was a Final Neolithic settlement of small rectangular rooms, home perhaps to 45-80 people, recently dated to about 4600-4500 BC."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kephala is on a rocky promontory [S2]; it is in the northern part of Kea [S2]; Kephala was a Final Neolithic settlement [S1, S3]; its buildings consisted of small rectangular rooms [S6]; it was perhaps home to 45-80 people (hedged) [S8]; it has recently been dated to about 4600-4500 BC [S7]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Kephala is a Final Neolithic settlement on a rocky promontory in northern Kea [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kephala,_Kea]; Kephala lies on a rocky promontory [SUPPORTED: https://www.ime.gr/chronos/01/en/nl/tn/kefala.html]; Kephala is in northern Kea [SUPPORTED: https://www.ime.gr/chronos/01/en/nl/tn/kefala.html]; Kephala belongs to the Final Neolithic [SUPPORTED: https://www.ime.gr/chronos/01/en/nl/tn/kefala.html]; The settlement consisted of small rectangular rooms [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kephala,_Kea]; Kephala was home to 45-80 people [SUPPORTED: https://www.ime.gr/chronos/01/en/nl/tn/kefala.html]; The population figure is an estimate (''perhaps'') [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kephala,_Kea]; Kephala has recently been dated to about 4600-4500 BC [SUPPORTED: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kephala,_Kea]"}]'::jsonb),
    ('2968fd35-9b61-4dfb-bb1b-cc4489827fd6'::uuid, 'card_description', 'Its Quechua name means big settlement: Hatunmarka, also spelled Hatunmarca, is an archaeological site in the Jauja Province of Junín, declared a National Cultural Heritage in November 2000.', 'The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage by Resolución Directoral Nacional No. 1346 in November 2000.', 'teaser-card-s002-rollback:2968fd35-9b61-4dfb-bb1b-cc4489827fd6:card_description', 'rollback of teaser-card-s002: card_description ''The site was declared a National Cultural Heritage by Resolución Directoral Nacional No. 1346 in November 2000.'' restored on Hatunmarka', '202f4aaa4f1b10ebd9c85bebb8e86902fbbdb44cf685a6bc0a9679a3b534cac6|838b2a217596bc7411430fab72ef68a5197f0cc1f65db4d0ac2181eac8f55b57|838b2a217596bc7411430fab72ef68a5197f0cc1f65db4d0ac2181eac8f55b57', '[{"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: writer teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-write-006", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Its Quechua name means big settlement: Hatunmarka, also spelled Hatunmarca, is an archaeological site in the Jauja Province of Junín, declared a National Cultural Heritage in November 2000."}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: checker teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-check-006 (check), PASS", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "the name is Quechua [S1]; the name means big settlement [S1]; it is also spelled Hatunmarca [S1]; Hatunmarka is an archaeological site [S1]; it is in the Jauja Province [S2]; it is in Junin (the Junin Region) [S2]; it was declared a National Cultural Heritage [S3]; the declaration was in November 2000 [S3]"}, {"source": "lane WB run wb-ws-2026-09-27-03: verifier teaser-wb-ws-2026-09-27-03-verify-017 (verify), VERIFIED", "url": "output/remediation/teaser/runs/wb-ws-2026-09-27-03/OUTCOMES.jsonl", "quote": "Hatunmarka is an archaeological site in the Jauja Province of Junín, Peru [SU